window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("reliability", {"topics":[{"key":"reliability/high-availability","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)"],"why":"A single server is a single point of failure: one disk, one kernel panic, one bad deploy, one\navailability-zone incident, and the product is down. The naive answer, \"buy a more reliable machine\",\nfails on arithmetic. A system is a chain of components, and if each one must be up for a request to\nsucceed, the chain's availability is the *product* of the parts: three dependencies at 99.9% each give\n0.999³ ≈ 99.7%, roughly 26 hours of downtime a year instead of 9. Adding components only makes this\nworse, and modern systems have dozens.\n\nHardware is also not the main enemy. Most outages in mature organisations are triggered by change:\ndeploys, configuration pushes, schema migrations, certificate rotations. The Google SRE book puts the\nshare of outages caused by changes to a live system at roughly 70%. A perfect machine does nothing for\nthat; what helps is being able to take any one instance out of service, including on purpose, without\nusers noticing. That is the real definition of HA in practice: *no single component is required*.\n\nFor AI systems the problem is sharper. Your request path includes an external model API with its own\nincidents, rate-limit spikes and regional capacity limits, plus GPU hosts that have more things to fail\nthan a commodity CPU box. If the provider is 99.9% and you promise 99.95%, you have promised something\nyou cannot deliver, no matter how many replicas of your own code you run.","how":"### Measuring it\nAvailability is either time-based (`uptime / total time`) or request-based\n(`successful requests / total requests`). Request-based is what users feel and what SLOs usually use,\nbecause a 10-minute outage at 3 a.m. and one at peak are not the same. The \"nines\" convert directly\nto a yearly budget (8,760 hours):\n\n```\n99%     → 87.6 h/year     99.9%  → 8.76 h/year\n99.99%  → 52.6 min/year   99.999% → 5.26 min/year\n```\n\nTwo composition rules do most of the reasoning. Components in **series** (all must work) multiply:\n`A = A₁ × A₂ × …`. Components in **parallel** (any one suffices, with perfect failover and independent\nfailures) compose as `A = 1 − (1 − A₁)(1 − A₂)…`, so two 99% replicas give 99.99%. The parallel formula\nis wildly optimistic whenever failures are correlated, and they usually are: both replicas run the same\ncode, received the same deploy, sit in the same zone, and call the same provider.\n\n### Removing single points of failure\n- **Stateless service tier.** Run at least two instances behind a load balancer, spread across\n  availability zones. Statelessness matters *because* it makes any instance interchangeable; session\n  state, conversation history and job progress move to replicated stores.\n- **Replicated state.** A database primary with a synchronous or asynchronous replica and automatic\n  promotion; a vector store with replicas; a cache that the service can live without.\n- **Redundant plumbing.** Managed load balancers and DNS are themselves redundant; a self-managed\n  single nginx box in front of a redundant fleet quietly undoes the fleet.\n- **Capacity headroom.** N+1 (or N+2) sizing, *because* a surviving replica that is already at 80%\n  cannot absorb its dead sibling's traffic; redundancy without headroom just moves the outage.\n\n### Detecting failure fast\nAutomatic failover is only as fast as detection. A health check that probes every 10 s and requires\n3 failures declares death after ~30 s, and that interval is pure downtime for traffic routed to the\ndead node. Checks must test the ability to serve, not just the ability to answer HTTP: a model server\nwhose GPU is wedged will happily return 200 from `/health` while every real request times out. The\nstandard split is a cheap *liveness* probe (restart me) and a *readiness* probe that reflects\ndependencies and warm-up (send me traffic). Readiness is what keeps a freshly started model server,\nstill loading weights, out of rotation.\n\n### Failover and its hidden costs\n**Active-passive** keeps a standby that takes over; failover costs detection time plus warm-up, and a\ncold standby that has to load a 70B-parameter model from object storage can take minutes to be\nuseful. **Active-active** has every node serving all the time, so a failure merely reduces capacity,\nand it doubles as proof that the second node actually works. In both cases in-flight work is lost:\na streaming generation dies with its node, so clients need retry logic and, for long agent runs,\ndurable checkpoints.\n\n### Change as the biggest availability risk\nBecause changes cause most outages, HA includes the deployment process: rolling updates that drain\nin-flight requests (the drain timeout must exceed your longest generation), canaries that take a\nfraction of traffic first, flags that can disable a feature without a deploy, and rollbacks that take\nseconds. A system that is redundant at runtime but deploys to all replicas at once is not HA.\n\n### Where AI systems differ\nModel providers are a shared dependency, so multi-provider fallback raises availability only if your\nprompts and evals are portable and you accept that the fallback model gives different answers:\n\"available but different\" is a product decision, not just an infrastructure one. GPU capacity is\nexpensive enough that N+1 redundancy may be replaced by graceful degradation to a smaller model, and\nfor self-hosted serving the readiness probe should include a real tiny inference.","example":"The script composes the availability of a chat product from its parts and shows how adding a second\nmodel provider moves the bottleneck rather than removing it. All inputs are illustrative targets, not\nmeasured figures, and the parallel rule assumes independent failures and perfect failover.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import reduce\n\ndef series(*parts):        # every part must be up\n    return reduce(lambda acc, a: acc * a, parts, 1.0)\n\ndef parallel(*parts):      # up if any part is up (independent failures, perfect failover)\n    return 1 - reduce(lambda acc, a: acc * (1 - a), parts, 1.0)\n\ndef downtime_hours_per_year(a):\n    return (1 - a) * 8760\n\nlb, api_replica, vector_db, provider = 0.9999, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.999\n\nsingle = series(lb, parallel(api_replica, api_replica), vector_db, provider)\ndual   = series(lb, parallel(api_replica, api_replica), vector_db, parallel(provider, provider))\n\nfor label, a in ((\"one provider\", single), (\"two independent providers\", dual)):\n    print(f\"{label}: {a:.4%}  ->  {downtime_hours_per_year(a):.1f} h/year\")\n# one provider:               99.8400%  ->  14.0 h/year   (provider dominates)\n# two independent providers:  99.9398%  ->   5.3 h/year   (vector DB now dominates)\n```\n\nWith one provider the whole product is pinned near 99.84% even though the API tier is 99.9999%. Adding\na second provider lifts it to ~99.94%, and now the single vector database is the weakest link, which\nis exactly where the next engineering dollar should go. If the two providers share a failure mode\n(same upstream GPU cloud, your own shared gateway), the real number sits between the two lines.","tradeoffs":["Every extra nine roughly multiplies cost and complexity, so a 99.9% target with fast rollback is often the right call for internal tools; it flips to 99.99%+ when revenue or safety rides on each request.","Active-active uses the standby capacity all the time and proves it works, but forces you to solve state sharing between nodes; active-passive is simpler and wastes the standby until the day it fails.","Multi-provider fallback raises model availability only when prompts, tool schemas and evals are portable; if outputs must be byte-stable, the fallback is a degraded mode, not a transparent one.","Aggressive health checks fail over faster but misdiagnose slow nodes as dead under load, which turns a latency problem into a capacity problem as traffic piles onto fewer survivors."],"pitfalls":["Counting redundancy that shares a failure mode: two replicas in one zone, two providers behind one gateway, two databases on one deploy pipeline. The symptom is \"redundant\" components failing together.","Health checks that test the process, not the service; the node stays in rotation while every real request fails, and the load balancer keeps sending it traffic.","No headroom: N replicas each at 80% load, so losing one overloads the rest and the \"failover\" is a cascading failure. Visible as a second outage minutes after the first node died.","Promising an SLA above the provider's: the math makes it impossible, and the symptom is a chronic error-budget deficit nobody can engineer away.","Forgetting that deploys are downtime: rolling restarts that kill in-flight generations show up as a spike of client errors at every release, not as an \"outage\"."],"when":"Decide the target before the design. Ask what one hour of downtime costs, convert the answer into a\nnumber of nines, and then pay for only that: an internal retrieval tool may be fine at 99.5% with a\n15-minute rollback, while a customer-facing assistant inside a checkout flow needs multi-zone\nactive-active and multi-provider fallback. Measure availability the way users experience it\n(successful requests, by endpoint, by tenant), not by host uptime. Day to day this shows up as\nreadiness probes, drain timeouts, N+1 capacity reviews and the question \"what happens if this box\ndisappears right now?\" in design reviews. In interviews, expect to compose availabilities, explain why\nthe provider caps yours, and distinguish HA (surviving a component failure with no visible downtime)\nfrom disaster recovery (restoring service after a site or data loss, with a measured gap).","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements readiness probes that run a real inference, retry-with-fallback on provider errors, and drain timeouts longer than the maximum generation time.","architect":"Sets the availability target per surface, owns the dependency availability math, and decides between active-active, active-passive and degradation-instead-of-redundancy for GPU tiers.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"five nines\" request into the provider's actual SLA and a multi-provider or self-hosted design, and documents the residual gap honestly."},"check":{"q":"A team promises 99.95% availability for an assistant whose path is: global load balancer\n(99.99%), three stateless API replicas across zones (99.9% each), one managed vector database\n(99.95%), and a single model provider (99.9%). Can they hit the target, and what are the first two\nchanges you would make?","a":"No. The three replicas in parallel are effectively 100%, but the series product\n0.9999 × 0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 99.84%, roughly 14 hours a year, is already below target before counting\ndeploys and their own bugs. The provider is the dominant term, so the first change is an independent\nsecond provider with portable prompts and evals, lifting the model term toward 99.9999% and the product\nto about 99.94%. The vector database then dominates; the second change is either a replica with\nautomatic failover or a degraded mode (answer without retrieval, flagged) so a vector outage is not a\nproduct outage. A strong answer adds that correlated failures make these numbers optimistic and that\nthe target should be renegotiated unless both changes are funded."},"quiz":[{"q":"Two API replicas each at 99.9% sit behind a load balancer. Why is the combined availability usually worse than the 99.9999% the parallel formula predicts?","options":["The load balancer halves the effective capacity of each replica","Their failures are correlated: same deploy, same zone, same dependencies, so they fail together","Parallel composition only applies to databases, not stateless services","Health checks cannot distinguish between two identical replicas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The parallel formula assumes independent failures. Replicas built from the same code and deployed together share most failure modes, so the real figure sits far closer to a single replica's."},{"q":"A model server loads weights for three minutes after start-up. Which probe design keeps it from receiving traffic too early without causing restart loops?","options":["A single liveness probe with a 10-second timeout","No probes; the load balancer retries failed requests automatically","A cheap liveness probe plus a readiness probe that only passes once a test inference succeeds","A readiness probe that checks the process is listening on its port"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Readiness gates traffic, liveness gates restarts. A liveness probe that fails during warm-up would kill the pod repeatedly; a port check passes before the model can actually answer."},{"q":"What distinguishes high availability from disaster recovery?","options":["HA masks single-component failures with no visible downtime; DR restores service after a site or data loss, accepting a measured gap","HA applies to stateless services and DR applies only to databases","HA is about hardware failures while DR is about software bugs","They are the same practice measured over different time windows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HA keeps the system up through routine faults using redundancy and failover. DR plans for the cases redundancy cannot cover, such as a region loss or corrupted data, and is measured by RPO and RTO."}]},{"key":"reliability/fault-tolerance","sources":["Avižienis, Laprie, Randell, Landwehr, 2004 — Basic Concepts and Taxonomy of Dependable and Secure Computing","Gray, 1985 — Why Do Computers Stop and What Can Be Done About It?","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"Components fail at rates that cannot be engineered away. A fleet of 1,000 disks with a 1% annual\nfailure rate loses about ten a year; a service making 10 million remote calls a day at a 0.01% failure\nrate sees a thousand failures daily. Distributed systems add a failure mode local code never has: a\nremote call can be lost on the way out, lost on the way back, or merely slow, and the caller cannot\ntell which. Jim Gray's analysis of Tandem outages found software and operations, not hardware, were the\ndominant causes, which is why \"buy better hardware\" never solved the problem.\n\nThe naive alternative is `try: call() except: call()`. It fails in three ways. It retries faults that\nretrying cannot fix (bad input, auth errors), it duplicates side effects when the first call actually\nsucceeded but the reply was lost, and under a real outage it multiplies load on the thing that is\nalready struggling, turning a partial failure into a total one. Retries without timeouts, budgets and\nidempotency are not fault tolerance; they are fault amplification.\n\nAgentic AI systems make this urgent. A 20-step agent whose steps each succeed 99% of the time finishes\ncleanly only 0.99²⁰ ≈ 82% of the time. Most of the 18% are transient: a 429 from the provider, a tool\ntimeout, a malformed JSON response. Converting those into successes, without double-charging a card or\nsending an email twice, is precisely the fault-tolerance toolkit applied to LLM calls and tools.","how":"### Vocabulary that keeps designs honest\nAvižienis et al. separate **fault** (the underlying defect: a bug, a dead NIC, a provider incident),\n**error** (the incorrect internal state it produces: a timed-out call, a null field) and **failure**\n(the service no longer doing what it promised, as seen by users). Fault tolerance is the set of\nmechanisms that stop the chain between error and failure. Naming which link you are breaking is the\ndifference between a design and a hope.\n\n### Choose the fault model first\nYou tolerate what you design for. **Crash-stop**: a node dies and stays dead. **Crash-recovery**: it\ncomes back with or without its state, so you must handle duplicate work. **Omission and timing**: a\nmessage or reply is lost or late, which is why every remote call needs a timeout, because \"slow\" is\nindistinguishable from \"dead\" from the outside. **Byzantine**: a component returns well-formed but wrong\noutput. Classic systems rarely engineer against Byzantine faults, but an LLM returning a confident,\nschema-valid, wrong tool argument is exactly that, which is why output validation and approval gates\nbelong in the same toolbox.\n\n### The toolbox, in the order you apply it\n1. **Timeouts**, *because* a hung call holds a thread, a connection and often a GPU slot; nothing else\n   in the list can act until the call returns. Derive them from the latency budget end to end, not\n   per hop in isolation.\n2. **Bounded retries with backoff and jitter**, *because* transient faults (429, 503, connection reset)\n   usually clear within seconds, and jitter prevents every client from retrying in lockstep. Retry only\n   transient classes, cap attempts, and give retries a budget (the SRE book's rule of thumb: retries\n   should be a small fraction, around 10%, of total requests) so an outage cannot double your load.\n3. **Idempotency**, *because* retries are only safe if a repeated operation has the same effect as one.\n   A pure completion call is naturally idempotent apart from billing; a tool that sends money is not,\n   and needs an idempotency key the receiver deduplicates on.\n4. **Circuit breakers**, *because* once a dependency is clearly down, continuing to call it wastes your\n   latency budget and its recovery capacity; fail fast and probe occasionally.\n5. **Bulkheads**, *because* a failing dependency should exhaust its own pool of connections or\n   workers, not the shared pool that serves everything else.\n6. **Fallbacks and graceful degradation**, *because* \"what do we do instead\" is a product decision\n   made in advance: a cached answer, a smaller model, retrieval-only mode, a static \"try again\" message.\n   Each operation gets one explicit choice.\n7. **Replication and consensus for state**, *because* stateless retries cannot recover data; replicas,\n   quorum writes and leader election keep the truth alive when a node holding it dies.\n\n### Fail-fast, fail-safe, fail-operational\nA fail-fast component reports an error immediately rather than hanging, which protects callers'\nbudgets. A fail-safe system moves to a safe state on fault, such as an agent refusing to act when its\npolicy check is unavailable. A fail-operational system continues full service through the fault\nthrough redundancy. Most AI features should be fail-fast internally and fail-safe at action boundaries.\n\n### Detection and its own failure mode\nHeartbeats, health checks and error-rate thresholds detect faults, but every detector has false\npositives. Declaring a slow primary dead and promoting a replica while the old one is still writing\nproduces split brain. Fencing tokens or a single arbiter prevent two nodes from both believing they\nlead. Detection thresholds are therefore a trade between failover speed and the risk of acting on a\nwrong diagnosis.","example":"A provider call wrapped in the first six tools: an overall deadline, per-attempt timeout, retries only\non transient statuses, full-jitter backoff that honours `Retry-After`, and a fallback that degrades\ninstead of failing. It assumes the call itself has no side effects, so repeating it is safe.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nclass Transient(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised by the client on 408/429/5xx; carries Retry-After when the server sent one.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, status, retry_after=None):\n        self.status, self.retry_after = status, retry_after\n\ndef tolerant_complete(primary, fallback, req, *, budget_s=45, attempt_timeout_s=20,\n                      max_attempts=3, base_s=0.5, cap_s=8.0):\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + budget_s             # one budget for the whole operation\n    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            break\n        try:\n            return primary(req, timeout_s=min(attempt_timeout_s, remaining))\n        except Transient as e:                         # non-transient errors propagate: retrying cannot fix them\n            sleep = e.retry_after if e.retry_after is not None \\\n                else random.uniform(0, min(cap_s, base_s * 2 ** attempt))   # full jitter\n            if time.monotonic() + sleep >= deadline:\n                break\n            time.sleep(sleep)\n    # primary exhausted or budget spent: degrade visibly rather than fail\n    remaining = max(1.0, deadline - time.monotonic())\n    return fallback(req, timeout_s=remaining)          # smaller model, cached answer, or a typed \"unavailable\"\n```\n\nIn production the `fallback` result should be tagged (a header or a field) so the UI and your metrics\nknow the answer came from the degraded path, and the retry count should be exported so you can see a\nprovider wobble before it becomes an outage. For tool calls with side effects, add an idempotency key\nto `req` before the first attempt, never inside the loop.","tradeoffs":["Retries raise success rates for transient faults but add load exactly when the dependency is weakest; the balance flips to \"stop retrying\" once the error rate suggests a real outage, which is the breaker's job.","Redundant replicas tolerate crash faults but cost double and introduce consistency problems; for rarely used or cheap-to-recompute state, a fast restore beats a live replica.","Fail-safe defaults protect users when a policy or guard service is down, but they turn a guard outage into a feature outage; fail-open is acceptable only for guards whose absence is low-risk.","Tight timeouts free resources quickly but cut off legitimately slow responses such as long generations; the right value comes from the latency distribution, and streaming changes what \"timeout\" should mean.","Fallback models keep the product available but change answer quality and behaviour; if customers depend on consistency, the fallback must be labelled and measured separately in evals."],"pitfalls":["Retrying non-idempotent tool calls after a timeout: the first call succeeded, the reply was lost, and the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or charges that correlate with provider latency spikes.","Nested retries across layers (SDK retries × service retries × client retries) that multiply into tens of attempts per user action; visible as request volume spiking several-fold during an incident.","Timeouts that add up: five sequential hops each with a 30 s timeout cannot meet a 30 s end-to-end promise, so users see failures even though every hop \"met its timeout\".","Treating any exception as transient, so bad prompts or revoked API keys are retried for minutes; the symptom is latency without errors in logs until the budget finally expires.","Fallbacks that were never exercised: the secondary provider's key expired or its tool schema drifted, discovered only during the primary's outage. Chaos tests exist for this."],"when":"Every remote call needs the first three tools (timeout, bounded jittered retry on transient classes,\nidempotency for anything with side effects); that is baseline, not a design choice. Add breakers and\nbulkheads when a dependency's failure can starve shared resources, and define a fallback for every\nuser-facing operation where \"nothing\" is worse than \"something degraded\". Do not add fault tolerance\nyou cannot observe: export attempt counts, breaker state and fallback usage, or you will not know the\nsystem is quietly running on its backup path. In day-to-day work it shows up as the client wrapper\neveryone imports and the FMEA row that says what happens when each dependency fails. In interviews,\nthe expected depth is explaining why retries need budgets and idempotency, and walking a fault from\ndetection to degraded response.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the shared provider and tool client: deadlines, jittered retries on transient statuses, idempotency keys for side-effecting tools, and metrics for every degraded path.","architect":"Chooses the fault model and the degradation ladder per capability, and sets the rule that no retry layer exists without a budget and no action tool exists without idempotency.","fde":"Maps which of the customer's systems can be retried safely, which need idempotency keys or reconciliation, and what degraded mode the customer's operations team will accept."},"check":{"q":"An agent's \"create purchase order\" tool call timed out at 30 seconds. The orchestrator retried,\nand the customer's ERP now shows two orders. Walk through what went wrong and how the design should\nchange at each layer.","a":"The timeout is an omission fault: the caller cannot know whether the first call completed, and\nin this case it did. Retrying a non-idempotent operation after an ambiguous failure is the root\nmistake. Fixes, from the inside out: the tool must accept an idempotency key generated once per\nintended action, before the first attempt, and the ERP adapter must deduplicate on it or query for an\nexisting order with that key before creating. The orchestrator should classify timeouts on\nside-effecting tools as \"unknown outcome\" and reconcile (read-after-timeout) rather than blindly\nretry. The 30 s timeout should be derived from the ERP's actual latency distribution so it does not\ntrip on normal slow paths. Finally, an approval gate or a daily reconciliation report catches the\ncases that still slip through, and the duplicate rate becomes a tracked metric."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must retries be combined with idempotency rather than used alone?","options":["Idempotency makes the retried call faster because the server can cache it","After a timeout the caller cannot know whether the first attempt took effect, so a repeat must be harmless","Providers reject retries that do not carry an idempotency header","Idempotency allows retries to skip the backoff delay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lost reply looks identical to a lost request. Without idempotency, retrying after an ambiguous failure duplicates side effects; with it, the second attempt either no-ops or returns the first result."},{"q":"A dependency starts returning 503s for 40% of calls. Which combination best protects the rest of the system?","options":["Increase the retry count to five and remove the timeout so slow calls can finish","Switch all calls to synchronous mode so failures are detected sooner","Keep a short timeout, retry within a budget, open a circuit breaker if errors persist, and serve a defined fallback","Disable the dependency's health check so the load balancer keeps traffic flowing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"More retries and no timeout pour load onto a struggling dependency and tie up your own resources. Budgeted retries plus a breaker contain the fault, and the fallback keeps users served."},{"q":"Which fault model best describes an LLM returning a syntactically valid tool call with the wrong account ID?","options":["Crash-stop, because the model stopped doing useful work","Omission, because the correct answer was omitted","Byzantine, because the component produced well-formed but incorrect output that passes shallow checks","Timing, because the model answered too quickly to have reasoned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Byzantine faults are wrong-but-plausible outputs from a component that has not crashed. They are caught by validation against ground truth and by approval gates, not by retries or timeouts."}]},{"key":"reliability/multi-region-architecture","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Abadi, 2012 — Consistency Tradeoffs in Modern Distributed Database System Design (PACELC)","Helland, 2007 — Life beyond Distributed Transactions","Official docs: AWS Well-Architected Framework, Reliability Pillar"],"why":"A single region, even spread across availability zones, shares a control plane, a set of regional\nservices and a blast radius. Regional incidents are rare but they do happen, they take down every zone\nat once, and the first thing a customer's procurement team asks after one is \"what is your multi-region\nstory\". Redundancy inside the region cannot answer that, because it is correlated failure by design.\n\nPhysics is the second reason. Light in fibre travels roughly 200,000 km/s, about 5 ms per 1,000 km one\nway, so a user 6,000 km from your only region pays at least ~60 ms of round trip before any work\nhappens, and in practice more after routing. For a chat product whose time-to-first-token budget is a\nfew hundred milliseconds, that is a visible slice.\n\nLaw is the third. Data-residency obligations require that certain customers' data is stored and\nprocessed inside a jurisdiction, and for an AI product \"processed\" includes the prompt sent to the\nmodel, so the inference endpoint must also be in-region.\n\nAI systems add a fourth, practical reason: model providers publish quotas and model availability per\nregion. A second region can double your effective rate limit and give you a place to run when one\nregion's capacity for a given model is exhausted. The naive approach, \"deploy the same Terraform to\ntwo regions and put DNS in front\", fails the moment two regions both accept writes to the same\nrecord, or a failover promotes a replica that is seconds behind. Compute is easy to duplicate; truth\nis not.","how":"### Three topologies, in rising cost\n1. **Active-passive.** One region serves; the other holds replicated data and enough infrastructure to\n   take over (from \"pilot light\" to \"warm standby\"). This is disaster recovery with a shorter clock,\n   covered in that lesson; the standby's real risk is that it is never exercised.\n2. **Active-active, partitioned.** Every region serves traffic, but each tenant, user or shard has a\n   single *home* region that owns its writes; the other regions hold async replicas and can take over\n   ownership on failover. This is the workable default for stateful products, *because* it keeps\n   every write single-leader and avoids write conflicts entirely while still using all regions.\n3. **Active-active, shared.** Any region accepts any write to any record. This needs either a\n   globally consistent database that pays cross-region latency on commits or multi-leader replication\n   with conflict resolution. Choose it only for data that is genuinely written from everywhere.\n\n### State is the whole problem\nA write replicated **synchronously** is durable in both regions before the client gets an\nacknowledgement, so failover loses nothing (RPO ≈ 0), *but* every write now waits a cross-region\nround trip, commonly tens of milliseconds within a continent and ~100 ms or more across an ocean\n(illustrative orders of magnitude). A write replicated **asynchronously** returns immediately, and the\nreplica trails by a lag that is usually sub-second but spikes under load; failover during a spike\nloses that window. PACELC names the trade honestly: under a partition you choose availability or\nconsistency, and even without one you choose latency or consistency.\n\nFor partitioned active-active, the mechanics are: route each request to the tenant's home region;\nreplicate asynchronously to the others; on failover, promote the replica, re-home the tenant, and\naccept the lag as lost writes to reconcile later. For shared active-active, conflicts must be resolved\nwith per-key ownership, CRDTs for data that merges naturally (counters, sets), or last-writer-wins\nwhere losing an update is tolerable. Helland's advice still holds: design entities so that each\ntransaction touches one entity in one place.\n\n### Routing and failover\nLatency-based or geo DNS sends users to the nearest healthy region, but DNS answers are cached and\nclients routinely ignore short TTLs, so DNS failover takes minutes. Anycast global load balancers move\ntraffic in seconds and are preferred where available. The dangerous moment is the decision itself:\nautomatic failover of a *database* on a flaky health signal produces split brain, two primaries both\naccepting writes. Mature teams automate stateless failover and keep data-tier failover behind a\nhuman-confirmed runbook step or a strict fencing mechanism.\n\n### Capacity is part of the design\nIf two regions each run at 70% utilisation and one dies, the survivor needs 140%. Either run each\nregion at or below 50% (paying for idle capacity) or plan explicit load shedding on failover:\nwhich tenants and which workloads keep running. The same arithmetic applies to provider quotas, which\ndo not move with your traffic.\n\n### What is different for AI systems\n- Model availability and rollout timing vary by region; the same model name may not be live in both\n  regions on the same day, so pin versions and test in both.\n- Embeddings must come from the same model and version in every region, or a replicated vector index\n  becomes incoherent. Replicate the index artefact or the source documents plus a rebuild pipeline,\n  and know how long rebuild takes.\n- Prefix caches and KV caches are per-server; a failed-over region starts cold and pays higher\n  time-to-first-token until warm.\n- Residency pins both storage and inference: a residency-bound tenant whose home region fails must\n  *degrade in place*, not be served from a foreign region.\n- Long-running agent runs in flight die with the region unless their progress is checkpointed in a\n  replicated store, which is why durable execution and multi-region go together.","example":"A worked failover for a two-region, tenant-partitioned agent platform (all numbers illustrative).\n\nSetup: regions `us-east` and `eu-west`, each with a full stateless tier, a Postgres primary for its\nhome tenants and an async replica of the other region's tenants with typical lag under 2 s. Agent\ncheckpoints live in the same database. Each region runs at about 40% of capacity. The provider quota\nis 1,000 requests per minute per region, and EU tenants are residency-bound.\n\nIncident: `us-east` becomes unreachable. The global load balancer marks it unhealthy after ~60 s of\nfailed checks and routes all users to `eu-west`. The on-call engineer confirms the region, not the\nchecks, is the problem and runs the promotion step; `eu-west`'s replica of US tenants becomes primary\nat T+6 min. Writes that `us-east` accepted in its last ~2 s before the loss never replicated: the RPO\nfor US tenants is about 2 s, and those writes appear in an idempotency log that reconciliation will\nreplay later. Agent runs that were mid-step in `us-east` resume in `eu-west` from their last\ncheckpoint, re-executing only the interrupted step.\n\nCapacity: `eu-west` now carries both regions' traffic, 40% + 40% = 80% of one region, so compute\nholds. The provider quota does not: EU's 1,000 rpm must now cover roughly double the demand, so the\nplatform sheds batch summarisation jobs first and keeps interactive chat. EU tenants are unaffected\nand remained in-region throughout. Had the failure gone the other way, residency-bound EU tenants\nwould have been placed in a read-only degraded mode rather than served from `us-east`.\n\nFailback: when `us-east` returns, replication is reversed, the reconciliation log is replayed, and\nUS tenants are re-homed during a low-traffic window after the replica catches up.","tradeoffs":["Region loss survival and lower latency for distant users, paid for with duplicated infrastructure and idle headroom; it stops paying off when all users and regulators sit in one geography.","Synchronous replication gives zero data loss but taxes every write with a cross-region round trip; flip to asynchronous when write latency is user-visible and a few seconds of loss is survivable.","Partitioned active-active avoids write conflicts but makes cross-tenant features (global search, shared workspaces) hard; shared active-active enables them at the price of conflict resolution.","Automatic failover shortens outages but risks split brain on false positives; manual data-tier promotion is slower and safer, and becomes the better choice as the data's value rises.","A second region doubles provider quota and model-availability options, yet model and prompt behaviour must now be validated in two places, doubling the eval and rollout work."],"pitfalls":["A standby region that was never failed over to: expired credentials, missing secrets, a model not enabled in that region. The symptom is discovering it during the real incident.","Replicating the database but not the vector index, prompt registry or feature flags, so the failed-over region answers from stale or empty retrieval and behaves differently.","Assuming DNS TTLs are honoured; traffic keeps arriving at the dead region for minutes after \"failover\" and users see errors you believe are impossible.","Treating replication lag as zero: dashboards show success, but customers report lost messages or duplicated agent actions from the seconds before failover.","Sizing each region for its own load; after failover the survivor overloads and a regional outage becomes a global one, visible as a second wave of errors minutes after the first."],"when":"Go multi-region when you can name the driver: a contractual requirement to survive region loss, a\nlatency budget that one region cannot meet for a material user base, a residency rule, or provider\nquota you cannot obtain in one region. Otherwise multi-AZ plus a tested disaster-recovery plan is\ncheaper and usually adequate. If you do go, start with partitioned active-active and asynchronous\nreplication, and reserve synchronous or globally consistent stores for the few records that truly\nneed them. Measure replication lag, failover time and survivor utilisation, and rehearse failover on\na schedule. In interviews, the signal of depth is talking about where writes are owned and what is\nlost on failover, not about load balancers.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes every service region-aware (home-region routing, region-tagged idempotency keys, checkpointed long runs) and runs the failover rehearsal that proves the second region works.","architect":"Decides the topology, which data is single-leader versus replicated, the consistency model per store, and the capacity and quota headroom each region must hold for failover.","fde":"Maps the customer's residency and latency constraints to tenant home regions, and sets expectations about what degrades, rather than moves, when a residency-bound region fails."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI assistant in `us-east` and `eu-west` with \"no data loss on failover\" and\n\"no added latency\". Both are stated as hard requirements. How do you respond, and what design do you\npropose?","a":"The two requirements conflict for any write: zero loss on region failure requires the write to\nbe durable in both regions before acknowledgement, which costs a cross-region round trip, tens of\nmilliseconds at best, on every write. So I would make the trade explicit and split the data. For the\nsmall set of records where loss is unacceptable, such as billing events or approval decisions, use\nsynchronous replication or a globally consistent store and accept the latency on those operations\nonly. For high-volume, user-visible writes like conversation turns and agent checkpoints, replicate\nasynchronously with sub-second lag, publish the measured RPO, and make the client idempotent so the\nfew seconds of lost writes can be replayed. Partition tenants by home region to avoid write conflicts,\npin residency-bound tenants to degrade in place, and rehearse the failover so the numbers are measured\nrather than promised."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a partitioned active-active design, why does each tenant have a single home region for writes?","options":["To reduce storage costs by keeping only one copy of each tenant's data","So every record has one leader at a time, eliminating cross-region write conflicts while all regions still serve traffic","Because cloud providers only permit one writable database per account","To guarantee that failover never loses any writes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Single-leader-per-tenant keeps writes conflict-free without global coordination. Data is still replicated to other regions; failover can still lose the replication lag window."},{"q":"What is the main risk of fully automatic failover for a cross-region database primary?","options":["It makes the replication lag longer because the replica must be verified first","It violates data-residency rules in every jurisdiction","A false-positive health signal can promote the replica while the old primary is still accepting writes, producing split brain","It prevents the application tier from also failing over"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Health checks can fail for reasons other than region death. Two primaries both accepting writes diverge, and reconciling them is far worse than a few minutes of human-confirmed failover."},{"q":"Two regions each run at 70% utilisation. One fails. What is the most likely outcome without a prior plan?","options":["The survivor serves all traffic normally because compute autoscaling is instant","The survivor is driven to 140% of its capacity and overloads, turning a regional outage into a global one","Traffic automatically waits in DNS until the failed region returns","Provider quotas shift to the surviving region to absorb the load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Failover moves demand, not capacity. Either run each region at or below 50% or decide in advance which workloads to shed, because autoscaling and provider quotas do not react in time."}]},{"key":"reliability/disaster-recovery","sources":["Official docs: AWS Well-Architected Framework, Reliability Pillar","NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 — Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"High availability handles component failures with redundancy that is usually in the same place and\nusually replicating in real time. That is exactly what makes it useless for a whole class of disasters.\nSynchronous replication faithfully copies a `DROP TABLE`, a bad migration, or a ransomware encryption\nto every replica within milliseconds. A regional outage takes every zone down together. A leaked cloud\ncredential can delete the replicas along with the primary. For these, \"we have replicas\" is not an\nanswer; you need a copy that is separated in time (point-in-time history), in place (another region\nor account), and in trust (immutable, separately permissioned).\n\nThe naive alternative is nightly backups and good intentions. It fails on three counts. Nobody has\ntested the restore, so it takes a day and surfaces a missing dependency. The backup is up to 24 hours\nold, so a whole day of conversations, approvals or orders vanishes. And the backup covers the\ndatabase but not the vector index, the prompt registry, the fine-tuned adapter weights or the\nsecrets, so the \"restored\" system does not behave like the one that was lost.\n\nAI systems raise the stakes because they carry state that is expensive to recreate. A vector index\nover millions of chunks can be rebuilt from source documents, but rebuilding means re-embedding\nhundreds of millions of tokens, which has a cost in money and hours that must be counted in RTO.\nFine-tuned weights that were never checkpointed to durable storage are simply gone.","how":"### The two numbers, precisely\nPicture a timeline. The disaster strikes at T. The most recent recoverable state is at some point\nbefore T; the gap is the **recovery point**, and the RPO is the maximum gap you have agreed to\ntolerate. Service comes back at some point after T; that gap is the **recovery time**, and the RTO is\nthe maximum you have agreed to tolerate. The objectives are promises set with the business; the\nmeasured results of a real recovery or drill are the actuals (often written RPA and RTA), and the gap\nbetween objective and actual is the finding.\n\nBoth numbers follow from the mechanism:\n- RPO is bounded by **how often state is copied away**. Nightly snapshot: up to 24 h. Continuous\n  log shipping (write-ahead log archived every minute): about a minute. Synchronous cross-region\n  replication: approximately zero, at the cost described in multi-region.\n- RTO is a **sum**: time to detect, time to decide, time to provision or promote, time to restore\n  data and replay logs, time to re-point traffic, time to verify. Teams who only measure the restore\n  step are usually off by an hour of detection and decision.\n\n### The four strategies\nNamed roughly as the AWS guidance does, in rising cost and falling RPO/RTO:\n1. **Backup and restore.** Periodic backups to another region or account; rebuild infrastructure from\n   code on demand. RPO hours, RTO hours to a day. Cheapest; adequate for internal tools.\n2. **Pilot light.** Data is continuously replicated to the DR region; the minimum core (database\n   replica, container images, secrets) is always on, everything else is created at failover. RPO\n   minutes, RTO tens of minutes to hours.\n3. **Warm standby.** A scaled-down but fully functional copy runs continuously and is scaled up on\n   failover. RPO seconds to minutes, RTO minutes.\n4. **Multi-site active-active.** Both sites serve traffic; failover is a routing change. RPO near\n   zero with synchronous replication, RTO near zero. This is the multi-region pattern.\n\n### Mechanisms that make the numbers real\n- **Point-in-time recovery** from a base snapshot plus an archived log lets you restore to just before\n  a bad write, *because* a replica cannot un-apply a mistake but a log can be replayed up to a chosen\n  second.\n- **Immutable, isolated backups**: object-lock or vault policies, a separate account with separate\n  credentials, *because* a disaster that includes a compromised identity must not reach the backup.\n- **Infrastructure as code** so the DR environment can be created, not hand-built, during the worst\n  hour of the year.\n- **Restore drills** on a schedule, timed, with the resulting RTA and RPA recorded. The drill also\n  catches the quiet killers: a backup job that has been silently failing, a missing KMS key grant.\n- **Runbooks** that name who decides, how failover is triggered, and how failback happens, including\n  the reconciliation of writes that landed after the recovery point.\n\n### The AI-specific inventory\nList every store, decide for each whether it is *restored* or *rebuilt*, and estimate the time:\nconversation and session databases (restore); vector indexes (restore from index snapshot in minutes,\nor rebuild by re-embedding in hours); source documents in object storage (usually versioned, near-zero\nRPO); prompt and config registries (in git, trivially recoverable, but only if the deploy pipeline\nreads from git and not from a mutable store); fine-tuned weights and adapters (copied to a second\nregion as artefacts, since retraining is not a recovery plan); agent checkpoints and workflow\nhistories (restore, and accept that runs after the recovery point replay from an earlier step); eval\ndatasets and golden sets (restore; losing them silently breaks release gates). The embedding model\nversion must be recorded with the index, or a rebuild produces an index the query path cannot use.","example":"A worked RTO/RPO budget for a RAG assistant (all figures illustrative orders of magnitude).\n\nThe business sets RPO 15 minutes and RTO 4 hours. State: a 500 GB Postgres database (sessions,\ntenants, audit log), a vector index of 2 million chunks averaging 500 tokens each, 300 GB of source\ndocuments in versioned object storage, prompts and configs in git, one fine-tuned adapter.\n\nCurrent plan: nightly Postgres snapshot copied cross-region, no index snapshot, adapter stored only on\nthe training machine. Worst-case RPO is 24 hours, failing the 15-minute target. Estimated RTO: detect\nand decide 30 min, provision from infrastructure code 20 min, restore 500 GB snapshot roughly 45 min,\nrebuild the index by re-embedding 2M × 500 = 1 billion tokens, which at provider rate limits in the\nlow millions of tokens per minute is on the order of eight hours, then verify 15 min. Total about\n10 hours, failing RTO, and the adapter is unrecoverable.\n\nRevised plan: enable continuous WAL archiving to the DR region with a one-minute archive interval\n(RPO about 1 minute, meets 15), snapshot the vector index to the DR region every six hours (an index\nrestore of roughly 20 minutes replaces the eight-hour rebuild; index RPO of 6 h is acceptable because\ndocuments since then can be incrementally re-embedded in the background), copy the adapter and its\ntraining manifest to artefact storage in both regions. New RTO estimate: 30 + 20 + 45 + 20 + 15 ≈ 130\nminutes, inside 4 hours, with a quarterly timed drill to replace the estimates with actuals.","tradeoffs":["Shorter RPO means more frequent or continuous replication, which costs bandwidth, storage and sometimes write latency; it flips to \"nightly is fine\" when the data is cheaply re-derivable.","Shorter RTO means more infrastructure kept running idle (warm standby, active-active); it flips to backup-and-restore when an hour of downtime costs less than the standby's monthly bill.","Rebuilding derived state such as vector indexes saves storage but spends hours and provider tokens during the worst moment; snapshotting the index costs storage every day to save that one day.","Immutable, separately-owned backups protect against compromised credentials and bad automation but make routine cleanup and retention changes slower and more bureaucratic.","Automated failover shortens RTO but can trigger on false alarms and cause data loss or split brain; a human decision step adds minutes and removes that class of error."],"pitfalls":["Backups that are never restored: the job has been failing for months or the restore needs a key nobody has. The symptom is discovering it during the disaster; the cure is timed drills.","Replication mistaken for backup: a bad migration or deletion is faithfully copied to every replica within seconds, and no point-in-time copy exists to roll back to.","Recovering the database but not the rest of the inventory, so the restored assistant answers from an empty index or an older prompt and the incident continues as a quality incident.","Backups in the same account and region as production, reachable by the same credentials; one leaked key or one wrong Terraform destroy removes both.","Measuring RTO as the restore step only, forgetting detection, decision and traffic cut-over; the drill shows two hours where the plan said thirty minutes."],"when":"Every production system needs a DR plan; the choice is which strategy tier, and that follows from the\nbusiness impact analysis: what an hour of downtime and an hour of lost data cost for each capability.\nPick the cheapest tier that meets the agreed RPO and RTO, write down the inventory of state and whether\neach item is restored or rebuilt, automate the restore, and drill it at least quarterly with the\nactuals recorded. Reach for cross-region or cross-account immutable backups the moment the data has\ncompliance value or the threat model includes compromised credentials. Day to day, DR shows up in\ndesign reviews as \"where does this new store get backed up and who restores it\". In interviews, the\nexpected depth is deriving RPO from the replication mechanism and RTO as a sum, and explaining why\nreplicas are not backups.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds every new store to the backup inventory, records embedding and model versions with index snapshots, and writes the restore script that the drill actually runs.","architect":"Chooses the DR tier per capability from the agreed RPO/RTO, owns the state inventory and the restore-versus-rebuild decision for derived data like indexes.","fde":"Answers the customer's RPO/RTO questionnaire with measured drill results, and aligns the deployment's backups with the customer's own retention and residency requirements."},"check":{"q":"A team says \"our Postgres has a synchronous replica in another zone, so our RPO is zero and we\ndo not need backups.\" Where does this reasoning fail, and what would you add?","a":"The replica protects against the primary's hardware or zone failing, which is high availability,\nnot disaster recovery. It replicates every write, including the destructive ones: a mistaken\n`DELETE`, a bad migration, an application bug corrupting rows, or an attacker with the database\ncredential. After any of those, both copies are equally wrong within milliseconds, and the RPO for\nthat class of disaster is effectively infinite because there is no earlier state to return to. A\nregional outage or an account compromise can also take both. I would add base snapshots plus\ncontinuous WAL archiving to a separate region and account with immutable retention, giving\npoint-in-time recovery to any second before the bad event, and a timed quarterly restore drill so the\nRTO is a measured number. I would also extend the inventory beyond Postgres to the vector index,\nprompt registry and model artefacts."},"quiz":[{"q":"A system takes a full backup every night at 02:00. Data is destroyed at 13:00. What is the recovery point loss, and which objective does it test?","options":["13 hours, testing the RTO","11 hours, testing the RPO","24 hours, testing the RTO","Zero, because the backup can be replayed forward to 13:00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The last recoverable state is the 02:00 backup, so 11 hours of writes are lost; that gap is the recovery point. A full backup alone cannot be rolled forward; that requires archived logs."},{"q":"Why is a synchronous database replica not a substitute for backups?","options":["Replicas are always slower to restore than backups","Replicas cannot be placed in another availability zone","Replication copies destructive writes and corruption immediately, so there is no earlier state to recover to","Replicas increase RPO because they lag the primary"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A replica is a live copy of the present, including mistakes. Point-in-time recovery needs history: snapshots plus logs that let you stop just before the bad write."},{"q":"For a RAG system, why should the vector index be snapshotted even though it can be rebuilt from the source documents?","options":["Source documents cannot be backed up reliably","Rebuilt indexes return different search results from the original","Rebuilding means re-embedding every chunk, which can take hours and provider spend, directly inflating RTO","Vector databases do not support restoring from a snapshot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rebuild is a valid recovery path, but its duration is part of the recovery time. A snapshot restore turns hours of re-embedding into minutes, usually for modest storage cost."}]},{"key":"reliability/business-continuity","sources":["ISO 22301 — Security and resilience, Business continuity management systems","NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 — Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"Disaster recovery answers \"how do we bring the system back\". It does not answer the questions that\ndecide whether the business survives the hour in between: which functions must keep working, who is\nallowed to declare the failover, how customers are told, what staff do by hand while systems are down,\nand what happens when the thing that failed is not your system but a supplier you cannot restore. A\ncompany that restores its database in forty minutes but spends six hours finding the person with the\nauthority to approve the restore has a DR plan and no continuity.\n\nThe naive alternative is to treat continuity as an IT problem and buy more redundancy. It fails for\ndisruptions that redundancy cannot touch. A model provider deprecates the model your prompts were\ntuned on with ninety days' notice. A vendor changes its terms so your use case is no longer permitted.\nA regulator halts processing of a data category. A key engineer who holds the undocumented\nknowledge of why the system works leaves. None of these show up on an uptime dashboard, and all of\nthem stop a business function just as thoroughly as a region outage.\n\nAI products concentrate these risks. Many run a core function on a single external model, a single\nembedding model that the whole index depends on, and a small number of people who understand the\nevaluation set. Enterprise buyers know this, which is why \"describe your business continuity plan\"\nand \"what happens if your AI vendor is unavailable\" are standard lines in security questionnaires,\nand why a credible answer is often a precondition for the deal.","how":"### Business impact analysis first\nThe BIA is a ranked inventory of business functions, not systems. For each function record: what it\ndoes, who depends on it, the **maximum tolerable downtime** (how long before harm becomes\nunacceptable; some frameworks call it maximum acceptable outage), the resources and systems it needs,\nupstream suppliers, and the cost of an hour of disruption in money, compliance exposure and customer\ntrust. RPO and RTO for each supporting system are *derived* from this, which is why a BIA done\nhonestly often shrinks the list of things that need expensive recovery: most functions can wait a\nday, and a few cannot wait ten minutes.\n\n### Threat and dependency mapping\nFor each critical function, list how it can be disrupted and through which dependency: infrastructure\n(region, network), suppliers (model provider, vector database SaaS, identity provider), data\n(corruption, loss of access), people (key-person loss, mass unavailability), and external (legal,\nregulatory, reputational). The AI-specific entries are supplier concentration on one model vendor,\nmodel and API deprecation timelines, sudden pricing or quota changes, and the fact that prompts and\neval sets are intellectual property that may live in one person's head or one unbacked-up notebook.\nFMEA is the structured tool for this step.\n\n### Strategies per function, in a ladder\nEach critical function gets a sequence of fallbacks, cheapest and most automatic first:\n1. **Automatic technical fallback**: secondary provider, smaller self-hosted model, cached results.\n   Owned by engineering and covered by fault-tolerance mechanisms.\n2. **Degraded service**: retrieval-only answers, templated responses, longer queues, with customers\n   informed. Owned by product; the acceptable degradation is decided in advance, not in the incident.\n3. **Manual workaround**: humans perform the function with a documented procedure and the minimum\n   tooling, *because* the business function matters more than the automation that normally does it.\n4. **Suspension with communication**: the function pauses, customers and regulators are told what and\n   when, and the backlog plan is known.\n\n### Plans, roles and communication\nThe business continuity plan documents activation criteria (who may declare an event and on what\nevidence), roles with named deputies, a communication tree for staff, customers and regulators,\ndecision authority during the event, and the recovery and return-to-normal steps. It is written for\nsomeone reading it at 3 a.m. with the usual experts unavailable, which is a different document from\nan architecture diagram.\n\n### Testing and maintenance\nPlans decay. Tabletop exercises walk the team through a scenario on paper and surface missing\ndecisions; functional drills actually execute the fallback (switch provider in production for an\nhour, process a day's work manually for a sample). Each exercise ends with recorded findings and\nowners. Plans are reviewed when the architecture, suppliers or staffing change, and at least annually.\nISO 22301 formalises this lifecycle; regulated customers may ask for alignment with it.\n\n### Vendor exit as a continuity control\nFor every critical supplier, know the exit: portable prompts and evals that have been run on a second\nmodel, an export path for the vector index and its embedding-model version, contract clauses on\nnotice periods, and a tested procedure to switch. The exit plan is the BC answer to supplier failure,\nand it turns a model deprecation from a crisis into scheduled work.","example":"A worked continuity plan for an AI support-triage function (figures illustrative).\n\nThe BIA for a SaaS company lists \"classify and route inbound support tickets\" as a critical function:\nabout 300 tickets per hour at peak, a contractual first-response time of four hours for enterprise\ncustomers, and a maximum tolerable downtime of two hours before SLA penalties start. Dependencies: one\nmodel provider for classification, a vector database SaaS for similar-ticket retrieval, the ticketing\nsystem, and two engineers who maintain the prompts and the 1,200-item eval set. Derived targets: RTO\none hour for the triage service, RPO five minutes for the routing audit log.\n\nThreats recorded: provider outage or quota cut, provider model deprecation, vector SaaS outage, loss\nof both prompt maintainers, a prompt-injection incident that forces a halt.\n\nLadder for a provider outage: at T+0 automatic fallback to a second provider using prompts already\nvalidated against the eval set with a recorded accuracy gap; if that also fails, at T+10 minutes the\nservice degrades to keyword rules that route to a general queue with a banner telling agents triage\nis degraded; at T+30 minutes, if the backlog exceeds 150 tickets, the support lead activates the manual\nprocedure in which two agents triage from a shared view, which the BIA shows can sustain roughly half\nof peak volume, so the backlog grows at about 150 tickets per hour and enterprise tickets are pulled\nfirst to protect the four-hour SLA. Communication: the status page updates at T+15 minutes, enterprise\ncustomers with contractual notification clauses are emailed at T+60.\n\nFor deprecation risk: the second provider is exercised in production for one hour every month, and\nthe eval set lives in the repository with two named owners plus a documented onboarding runbook. The\nplan is tabletop-tested twice a year, and last year's drill found the fallback provider's key had\nexpired, which is now monitored.","tradeoffs":["A thorough BIA focuses spend on the functions that matter, but it takes real time from business owners; it flips to a lightweight version for small teams whose entire product is one function.","Manual workarounds keep the function alive without any system, yet they sustain only a fraction of volume; they are worth documenting when the function is critical and the volume is manageable.","Keeping a second model provider validated gives a real exit and fallback, at the cost of running evals twice and accepting behaviour differences; it stops paying when the function is non-critical.","Frequent drills keep plans honest but consume engineering and operations time; the balance tilts to more drills as contractual penalties and regulatory exposure grow.","Detailed plans cover more scenarios but go stale faster; shorter plans with clear roles and a decision tree survive turnover better than hundred-page binders."],"pitfalls":["Writing the plan for systems instead of functions, so the team restores the database while nobody handles the customers; the symptom is a \"successful recovery\" followed by churn and penalties.","Fallbacks that exist on paper only: the secondary provider was never run against the eval set, and its first production use reveals a large accuracy drop or broken tool schemas.","Undefined activation authority, visible as an hour lost to \"who can approve this\" while the backlog grows; name the roles and deputies in advance.","Single points of knowledge: one engineer understands the prompts and the eval set, and the plan silently assumes they are reachable. Key-person loss is a continuity event.","Ignoring supplier-side disruptions such as deprecations, quota cuts and terms changes because the dashboard is green; the symptom is a ninety-day deadline nobody planned for."],"when":"Do a business impact analysis the moment an AI feature supports a function someone depends on\ncontractually or operationally, and revisit it when you add a supplier, drop a human from the loop, or\nsign an enterprise customer with notification or recovery clauses. Keep the plan proportionate: a\none-page ladder with named roles beats a binder nobody reads. Measure drill findings closed, time to\nactivate the fallback, and the sustainable throughput of the manual mode, since those are the numbers\nauditors and customers will ask for. In day-to-day work this is the \"what do we do if the provider is\ndown for a day\" conversation and the vendor-exit checklist. In interviews, the distinguishing point is\nthat BC is function-first and includes people, suppliers and communication, while DR is the technical\nrestore underneath it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and exercises the automatic fallback tier, keeps the second provider's prompts and evals current, and makes the degraded mode observable and switchable without a deploy.","architect":"Owns the dependency map and supplier concentration risk, sets the degradation ladder per function, and translates the BIA's tolerable downtime into system RPO and RTO.","fde":"Writes the customer-facing continuity answer for questionnaires, aligns the plan with the customer's own BC programme, and runs the joint tabletop that proves both sides know their roles."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant's only model provider announces that the model you use will be retired in ninety\ndays, and its replacement scores noticeably lower on your eval set. Is this a business continuity\nevent, and how should a mature plan have prepared for it?","a":"Yes. Nothing is down, but a critical function faces a dated loss of its primary dependency with\na quality regression attached, which is exactly a supplier disruption in BIA terms. A mature plan\nwould already hold an exit path: prompts and tool schemas kept portable, a second provider or a\nself-hosted model evaluated on the same set with a recorded gap, and the eval set itself versioned\nwith named owners so the comparison can be rerun this week rather than rebuilt. The response is then\nscheduled work: run the eval against the replacement and the alternates, pick the best, tune prompts\nwith the gap measured, canary the switch, and update the ladder so the retired model is no longer a\nfallback. Communication to customers who contractually care about model changes happens on the\nplan's timeline. Without that preparation, the same notice becomes a ninety-day scramble and a\nsilent quality drop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the relationship between business continuity and disaster recovery?","options":["They are synonyms used by different industries","DR is the technical subset of BC that restores systems; BC also covers functions, people, suppliers and communication","BC is for small outages and DR is for large ones","DR is planned by the business and BC by engineering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Continuity asks how the function keeps running, including by hand or through a different supplier. Recovery asks how the system comes back. A fast restore with nobody handling customers is DR without BC."},{"q":"Where should the RTO for a system come from?","options":["The cloud provider's published recovery figures for the service","The engineering team's estimate of how fast a restore can run","The business impact analysis's maximum tolerable downtime for the functions that system supports","The average duration of past incidents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Objectives are requirements set by business impact, then met by engineering. Deriving them from what the system can already do inverts the logic and hides the gap the plan exists to close."},{"q":"Which of these is a business continuity risk that uptime monitoring will not detect?","options":["A model provider returning 503 errors for ten minutes","A database primary failing over to its replica","The one engineer who maintains the prompts and the evaluation set resigning","A load balancer health check marking a node unhealthy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Key-person loss stops a function's ability to change or recover safely without any system going down. BC treats people and knowledge as dependencies to be documented and duplicated."}]},{"key":"reliability/durable-workflow-execution","sources":["Official docs: Temporal","Garcia-Molina & Salem, 1987 — Sagas","Helland, 2007 — Life beyond Distributed Transactions"],"why":"Real processes span time and systems: draft a reply with a model, wait for a human to approve, send\nit, poll a vendor for confirmation, retry tomorrow if the vendor is down. A worker running that as one\nfunction dies halfway during a deploy, and the process is simply lost: the draft was made and paid\nfor, the approval request was sent, nobody knows which step completed.\n\nThe naive fix is a `status` column plus a cron job that scans for stuck rows and a retry loop around\neach step. Every team builds this once, and it grows the same bugs: steps that run twice because the\nstatus update raced with the crash, timers reimplemented as polling, \"waiting for approval\" rows that\nnobody sweeps, a state machine spread across three services with no single place to see where a\nprocess is, and no way to change the logic while thousands of processes are mid-flight. Message\nqueues help with retries but not with the orchestration: a queue cannot express \"wait 48 hours, then\nif no signal arrived, escalate\".\n\nAgentic systems make this acute. An agent run is a loop of model calls and tool calls, each of which\ncan take seconds to minutes, fail transiently, and cost real money. Re-running the whole loop after a\ncrash duplicates tool side effects and token spend; abandoning it loses the user's work. Durable\nexecution exists so that a crash at step 14 of 20 resumes at step 14 with steps 1 to 13 already\ndecided, without re-calling the model for any of them.","how":"### The core idea: event sourcing applied to a program's execution\nA durable workflow engine (Temporal and its predecessor Cadence, AWS Step Functions, Azure Durable\nFunctions, Restate, Inngest, DBOS and others differ in surface but share this model) records every\ndecision a workflow makes as an event: \"activity X scheduled\", \"activity X completed with result R\",\n\"timer T started\", \"signal S received\". The history lives in a durable store. Workflow code never\nholds state that matters only in memory; its memory is reconstructible from the history.\n\n### Replay\nWhen a worker crashes or is redeployed, another worker picks up the workflow by re-executing its code\nfrom the beginning *against the recorded history*. Each time the code reaches a step whose result is\nalready in the history, the engine returns the recorded result instead of running the step again.\nThe code therefore reaches the same point it was at, with identical local variables, and continues\nwith the first step that has no recorded outcome. This is why workflow code must be\n**deterministic**: no wall-clock reads, random numbers, direct network or database calls, or\niteration over unordered maps inside the workflow function, *because* a different decision during\nreplay would diverge from history and the engine would have to fail the workflow. Engines provide\ndeterministic substitutes (`workflow.now()`, seeded random, versioned patches).\n\n### Activities: where the real world lives\nAnything non-deterministic or side-effecting, including every LLM call, tool call, HTTP request and\ndatabase write, is an **activity**. Activities run outside the replay machinery, have timeouts and a\nretry policy, and their *results* are what gets recorded. The engine guarantees an activity is\nretried until it succeeds or the policy gives up, and that its result is never recomputed on replay.\nWhat the engine cannot guarantee is that an activity ran exactly once: a worker can complete the side\neffect and crash before reporting, and the retry runs it again. Activities must therefore be\n**idempotent**, with a key derived from the workflow and step identity, which is the same\nexactly-once limitation that applies to every distributed system.\n\n### Durable timers, signals and queries\n`sleep(30 days)` in a workflow is an event, not a blocked thread: the worker is free, and any worker\ncan resume the workflow when the timer fires, across deploys. **Signals** deliver external input, such\nas a human approval or a webhook, into a running workflow; **queries** read its current state without\naffecting history. Together they replace the cron-plus-polling pattern with code that reads as a\nstraight-line program.\n\n### Task queues and workers\nWorkflows and activities are dispatched through named task queues; workers poll them. This gives\nnatural bulkheads (GPU-bound activities on one queue, email on another), per-queue concurrency limits\nthat act as backpressure, and **heartbeats** for long activities so a dead worker is detected and its\nactivity rescheduled rather than waiting for the full timeout.\n\n### Living with long-running code\nBecause deploying new workflow code while old histories exist can break replay, engines offer\n**versioning** primitives: a patch marker that lets new code take the old branch for histories\nrecorded before the change. Very long or unbounded loops, like an agent that may take thousands of\nsteps, use **continue-as-new**, which closes the current history and starts a fresh one carrying\nforward only the needed state, *because* replaying a history of millions of events is slow and\nmemory-hungry.\n\n### Compared with the alternatives\nA saga implemented by hand gives compensation but not resumption; a queue gives retries but not\norchestration; a declarative state-machine service (Step Functions style) gives durability with\nJSON-defined states, which is excellent for fixed pipelines and awkward for branching agent logic.\nThe code-first engines trade that simplicity for ordinary programs with determinism rules.","example":"A Temporal-style workflow (Python SDK conventions; check names against the current docs) in which the\nLLM call and the send are activities, and the approval wait is a durable timer plus signal. A crash\nafter `draft_reply` completes resumes at the wait without calling the model again.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom temporalio import activity, workflow\nfrom temporalio.common import RetryPolicy\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def draft_reply(ticket_id: str) -> str:\n    return await llm.complete(f\"Draft a reply for ticket {ticket_id}\")       # your model client\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def send_reply(ticket_id: str, text: str) -> None:\n    # idempotency key = workflow identity + step, so an activity retry cannot send twice\n    await mailer.send(ticket_id, text, idempotency_key=f\"reply:{ticket_id}\")\n\n@workflow.defn\nclass TicketReplyWorkflow:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.approved: bool | None = None\n\n    @workflow.signal\n    def approve(self, ok: bool) -> None:          # delivered by the review UI\n        self.approved = ok\n\n    @workflow.run\n    async def run(self, ticket_id: str) -> str:\n        draft = await workflow.execute_activity(\n            draft_reply, ticket_id,\n            start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=90),\n            retry_policy=RetryPolicy(maximum_attempts=3, initial_interval=timedelta(seconds=2)),\n        )\n        try:   # durable wait: no worker is blocked, and it survives restarts and deploys\n            await workflow.wait_condition(lambda: self.approved is not None, timeout=timedelta(days=2))\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            return \"expired\"\n        if not self.approved:\n            return \"rejected\"\n        await workflow.execute_activity(send_reply, args=[ticket_id, draft],\n                                        start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=30))\n        return \"sent\"\n```\n\nNotice what is *not* in the workflow: no `datetime.now()`, no HTTP, no database. Those would be\nre-executed on replay. The model is called inside an activity precisely because it is\nnon-deterministic; its recorded result is what the replayed workflow sees.","tradeoffs":["Crash-proof resumption and readable straight-line orchestration, in exchange for determinism rules that are easy to violate; the trade pays off once a process has more than two steps or waits on humans or timers.","Every step's inputs and outputs are persisted, giving a complete audit trail, but large payloads (full documents, long transcripts) bloat history; pass references to blob storage instead.","Activity retries lift success rates, yet retrying an LLM activity re-spends tokens and may produce a different answer; make the activity idempotent on its own cache or accept the cost knowingly.","Workflow versioning lets you change logic under running instances, but each patch is permanent complexity until old histories drain; short-lived workflows can skip it, multi-week ones cannot.","Declarative state-machine services need no determinism discipline and integrate natively with cloud services; they become painful when the branching is data-dependent, as in agent loops."],"pitfalls":["Calling the model or a tool directly from workflow code; it works in tests and then replays into a second real call, visible as duplicated emails or doubled token bills after a worker restart.","Non-deterministic workflow code (`time.time()`, `uuid4()`, dict iteration order, reading config at runtime), which surfaces as workflows failing with non-determinism errors only after a deploy.","Activities without idempotency keys, so an at-least-once retry after a crash performs the side effect twice; the symptom is duplicates that correlate with deploys and worker restarts.","Unbounded agent loops in one history, discovered as slowly growing replay latency and memory until the workflow stalls; use continue-as-new on a step budget.","Missing heartbeats on long activities, so a dead worker's multi-minute inference is not rescheduled until the full timeout expires, inflating latency on every infrastructure hiccup."],"when":"Reach for durable execution when a process spans more than one side-effecting step, waits on timers\nor humans, or must survive deploys mid-flight: approvals, multi-step agent tasks, document pipelines,\nonboarding flows, anything the business would describe as \"a case\". Do not use it for a single\nrequest-response call or for pure data transformations where a queue and an idempotent consumer are\nenough. Measure workflow completion rate, time spent in each activity, retry counts per activity type\nand history size, and alert on non-determinism errors after deploys. In day-to-day work the pattern\nshows up as the discipline \"model and tools in activities, decisions in the workflow\". The\nagent-reliability module covers checkpoint stores and agent-specific recovery; this lesson is the\ngeneral runtime they rest on. In interviews, explaining replay and why determinism follows from it\nis the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes activities for every model and tool call with timeouts, retries and idempotency keys, keeps workflow code deterministic, and applies continue-as-new on long agent loops.","architect":"Chooses between a code-first engine and a declarative state machine, defines task-queue bulkheads and concurrency limits, and sets the payload and history-size policies.","fde":"Maps a customer's multi-day approval and escalation processes onto workflows with signals, and shows the history as the audit trail their compliance team asked for."},"check":{"q":"A team runs a 30-step research agent as one function inside a job queue with retries. After a\ndeploy, users report duplicated documents and bills roughly double for the hour. Diagnose it and\nexplain how durable execution changes the picture.","a":"The queue restarted the job from step one after the deploy killed the worker mid-run. Every\nmodel call and tool call before the crash point was repeated, so tokens were spent twice and\nnon-idempotent tool calls such as \"create document\" ran again. Retries at the job level are\nat-least-once for the whole job, which is the wrong granularity. Under durable execution each of the\n30 steps is an activity whose result is recorded in the workflow history as it completes. On restart\nthe workflow replays: steps one to the crash point return their recorded results without executing,\nso no tokens are re-spent and no tools re-run, and only the interrupted step is retried. That step\nstill needs an idempotency key, because the crash could have happened after the side effect and\nbefore the result was recorded. The team should also move the loop under a step budget with\ncontinue-as-new and add heartbeats to long model calls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must durable workflow code be deterministic?","options":["Because the engine executes it on a single thread for performance","Because recovery works by re-executing the code against the recorded history, and any divergence makes replay fail","Because non-deterministic code cannot be serialised to the database","Because activities require deterministic inputs to be retried"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replay reconstructs state by running the code again and substituting recorded results. If the code takes a different branch the second time, the history no longer matches and the engine cannot continue."},{"q":"Where should an LLM call live in a durable workflow, and why?","options":["In the workflow function, so its result is recorded in history","In a signal handler, so it runs only when triggered","In an activity, because it is non-deterministic and side-effecting; its recorded result is what replay sees","In a query handler, so it does not affect workflow state"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activities run outside replay, with timeouts and retries, and only their results enter the history. Calling the model in workflow code would re-invoke it on every replay and break determinism."},{"q":"An activity performs a payment and the worker crashes before reporting completion. What does the engine do, and what must the activity therefore guarantee?","options":["The engine detects the completed payment and records it automatically","The engine marks the workflow failed to avoid any risk of duplication","The engine retries the activity, so the activity must be idempotent to avoid charging twice","The engine rolls back the payment using the recorded history"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Durable execution gives at-least-once activity execution; the engine cannot see external side effects. Exactly-once effects come from idempotency keys the receiver deduplicates on, not from the engine."}]},{"key":"reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload; Addressing Cascading Failures)","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Nichols & Jacobson, 2012 — Controlling Queue Delay","Bronson et al., 2021 — Metastable Failures in Distributed Systems"],"why":"Little's law says the number of requests inside a system equals arrival rate times time spent:\n`L = λ × W`. A service that completes 50 requests per second and receives 60 accumulates 10 per\nsecond in its queue forever. After one minute, 600 requests are waiting, each facing a 12-second\nwait before service even starts. If the client timeout is 10 seconds, every one of those requests is\nalready dead on arrival, yet the server will still spend full effort serving them to clients that\nhave hung up and retried. Now the real arrival rate is 60 plus the retries. This is congestive\ncollapse: throughput of *useful* work (goodput) falls toward zero while the machines run at 100%.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"add a bigger queue\", makes it worse, *because* queue depth converts directly\ninto latency and buys nothing once wait time exceeds what callers will tolerate. \"Autoscale\" helps\nonly if new capacity arrives faster than the queue grows, which is rarely true for GPU-backed\nservices where a replica takes minutes to pull weights and warm up. Rate limiting by fixed quota\nhelps with abuse but not with legitimate surges, and it cannot see the server's real condition.\n\nThe system must therefore do two things the naive design does not: tell the source to slow down,\nand refuse work it cannot finish in time. LLM serving makes this concrete. A GPU server has a fixed\nnumber of sequences it can batch; beyond that, requests queue, time-to-first-token inflates, and\nusers retry or send the same prompt from another tab. The provider's own 429 responses are\nbackpressure aimed at you, and honouring them with backoff rather than retrying harder is the\ndifference between a slow hour and a dead one.","how":"### Backpressure: propagate the limit upstream\nBackpressure is the chain of signals that makes the fastest component in a pipeline run at the\nspeed of the slowest. Inside a process it is a bounded queue: a producer that calls `put()` on a full\nqueue blocks or fails instead of allocating more memory. Across a network it is TCP flow control\n(the receive window), HTTP 429 or 503 with `Retry-After`, gRPC `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, or a streaming\nprotocol in which the consumer pulls chunks at its own pace. In message systems it is consumer lag\nyou watch and a producer that pauses on it. The design rule is that every hop must have a bounded\nbuffer and a defined behaviour when it is full, *because* an unbounded buffer anywhere silently\nabsorbs the signal and the overload reappears there as memory exhaustion.\n\n### Load shedding: refuse early, refuse cheap\nShedding is the decision to drop or reject some requests to keep the others within their SLO. The\nmechanics that matter:\n- **Reject at the front door.** The cheapest point to refuse is before any expensive work: before\n  retrieval, before tokenisation, before taking a GPU slot. A rejection that costs a millisecond\n  preserves capacity; one that happens after ten seconds of queueing has already spent it.\n- **Use queue wait time as the overload signal**, not CPU. For a GPU service CPU is meaningless, and\n  for any service the quantity users feel is how long a request waited. Bound the queue by time:\n  if the oldest waiting request has been there longer than the budget allows, start shedding.\n- **Size the queue from the SLO**: `max_queue ≈ service_rate × max_acceptable_wait`. At 50 req/s\n  and a 2 s wait budget, a queue deeper than 100 only manufactures timeouts.\n- **Shed by priority.** Classify work as interactive, background or batch, by tenant tier, or by\n  whether it is a retry. Under pressure drop batch first, then background, and protect health checks\n  and control-plane traffic absolutely. Priority turns shedding from random failure into a policy.\n- **Prefer LIFO or CoDel under overload.** A FIFO queue serves the oldest request, which is the one\n  most likely to have been abandoned. Serving newest-first, or dropping when the minimum sojourn\n  time exceeds a target for a sustained interval (CoDel), keeps goodput up during the surge.\n- **Adaptive concurrency limits.** Instead of a fixed limit, measure latency and raise the limit while\n  latency stays flat, lower it when latency rises (gradient or TCP-Vegas-style controllers). This\n  tracks the service's real capacity as model sizes, prompt lengths and downstream speed change.\n\n### Where retries fit\nRetries are a load multiplier. A client that retries three times turns a 10% overload into a 40%\none. Shedding only works if clients back off on 429/503 with jitter, honour `Retry-After`, and keep a\nretry budget. Circuit breakers on the client side stop the storm entirely once rejection rates are\nhigh; bulkheads keep one overloaded dependency from consuming every worker.\n\n### Goodput, not throughput\nThe metric that tells you whether this is working is successful requests completed within the SLO per\nsecond. A server reporting 100% utilisation and 50 req/s throughput while 90% of responses arrive\nafter the client gave up has a goodput near zero. Dashboards should show queue wait distribution,\nrejection rate by priority, and goodput side by side.\n\n### LLM-specific shape\nCapacity is in concurrent sequences and tokens, not requests, so admission control should estimate\ncost (prompt length, requested max tokens) and admit by a token budget. Streaming adds a second\nbackpressure path: a slow consumer of server-sent events does not slow generation, so the server\nneeds a bounded per-connection buffer and cancellation on disconnect, or it generates tokens nobody\nwill read. Long generations hold their slot for the whole duration, so a few multi-minute requests\ncan starve interactive ones unless they are in a separate pool.","example":"Admission control for a chat endpoint: shed at the door by priority, bound the queue wait by the\nSLO, release the slot if the client left while waiting. The limits would be derived from measured\nservice rate and the latency budget, then adjusted by an adaptive controller.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request, Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\nMAX_INFLIGHT, MAX_QUEUE, MAX_WAIT_S = 32, 64, 2.0   # capacity, service_rate x wait budget, SLO slice\nslots = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_INFLIGHT)\nwaiting = 0                                          # single event loop: no lock needed\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(req: Request):\n    global waiting\n    priority = req.headers.get(\"x-priority\", \"interactive\")\n    batch_cap = MAX_QUEUE // 4                       # batch work gets a quarter of the queue\n    # 1) shed at the door: a 1 ms rejection instead of a 10 s timeout\n    if waiting >= MAX_QUEUE or (priority == \"batch\" and waiting >= batch_cap):\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"overloaded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"5\"})\n    waiting += 1\n    try:\n        # 2) bounded queue wait: beyond the budget the answer would arrive too late to matter\n        await asyncio.wait_for(slots.acquire(), timeout=MAX_WAIT_S)\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"queue wait exceeded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"5\"})\n    finally:\n        waiting -= 1\n    try:\n        if await req.is_disconnected():              # 3) client gave up while queued: do not spend the GPU\n            return Response(status_code=204)\n        return await run_inference(await req.json()) # your model client; token-cost admission goes here\n    finally:\n        slots.release()\n```\n\nWalk the numbers: if inference averages 640 ms per request with 32 slots, service rate is about\n50 req/s; a 64-deep queue therefore represents roughly 1.3 s of wait, inside the 2 s budget, and\nanything beyond is rejected in microseconds with a `Retry-After` the client should honour. Export\n`waiting`, rejections by priority and queue wait time, and the picture of an overload becomes a\nflat goodput line instead of a cliff.","tradeoffs":["Shedding sacrifices some requests to keep latency bounded for the rest, which is right whenever a late answer is worthless; it flips to queueing when callers will genuinely wait, as in batch jobs.","Blocking backpressure keeps every request eventually served but stalls producers and can deadlock if a producer also holds resources the consumer needs; explicit rejection is safer across services.","Fixed concurrency limits are predictable and easy to reason about but go stale as prompts, models and dependencies change; adaptive limits track reality at the cost of a controller to tune.","LIFO serving maximises goodput under overload but starves old requests and breaks fairness; use it only during overload and return to FIFO when the queue drains.","Priority shedding protects the work that matters most but needs honest classification; once every team marks its traffic \"critical\", the mechanism degrades to random drops."],"pitfalls":["Unbounded queues anywhere in the path (an async job queue, an executor pool, a channel), visible as latency rising for minutes before a memory crash instead of a quick, clean rejection.","Shedding after the expensive work: rejecting at the model call after retrieval and prompt assembly already ran, so the server stays saturated while returning errors.","Clients that treat 429 and 503 as transient and retry immediately without backoff, so every shed request returns within milliseconds and arrival rate doubles; the symptom is rejections climbing faster than traffic.","Using CPU or request count as the overload signal for GPU-bound work; the GPU is saturated at 20% CPU and queue wait time was the signal all along.","Health checks sharing the shed queue, so under load the load balancer marks healthy nodes dead and removes capacity exactly when it is needed, turning overload into an outage."],"when":"Every service with a finite capacity needs both: bounded buffers with a defined full behaviour at\nevery hop, and an admission decision at the front door driven by queue wait time and priority. Start\nby measuring service rate and the latency budget, set queue bounds from them, and add adaptive limits\nonce prompt lengths or model mix vary. Choose blocking backpressure inside a process or pipeline,\nexplicit 429/503 across service boundaries, and always pair shedding with client backoff and retry\nbudgets. Watch goodput, queue wait percentiles and rejection rate by priority; if rejections never\nhappen, the limits are either generous or missing. In day-to-day work this is the semaphore in front\nof the model client and the `Retry-After` handling in the SDK. In interviews, the depth signal is\nderiving queue size from Little's law and explaining why early rejection raises goodput.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Puts a bounded semaphore and a queue-wait timeout in front of every model and tool client, honours provider `Retry-After`, and exports goodput and rejection metrics by priority.","architect":"Defines the priority classes and tenant tiers that shedding enforces, sets latency budgets that queue bounds derive from, and places bulkheads so one workload cannot starve another.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their burst traffic sees 429s rather than slow answers, tunes priorities to their business rules, and sizes capacity against their measured peak arrival rate."},"check":{"q":"A GPU-backed summarisation service handles about 40 requests per second with p50 latency under\na second. A marketing email sends 90 requests per second for ten minutes. Clients time out at 8\nseconds and retry twice. Describe what happens without protection, then design the protection and\njustify each number.","a":"The queue grows by 50 requests per second, so after 8 seconds the oldest waiting request has\nalready exceeded the client timeout; from then on the server serves abandoned requests while clients\nadd two retries each, pushing effective arrival toward 270 per second. Goodput falls near zero though\nthe GPUs are fully busy, and the collapse can outlast the ten-minute surge because retries keep it\nfed, the metastable pattern. Protection: bound the queue by wait time, around 40 × (8 s minus\nservice time), so no admitted request can miss its deadline; shed above that at the front door with\n503 and `Retry-After`; classify the campaign's traffic as batch so it gets a small share and\ninteractive users stay protected; require the client SDK to back off with jitter and cap retries at a\nbudget; add an adaptive concurrency limit so the bound follows real latency; and alert on goodput and\nrejection rate rather than utilisation. The campaign then completes slower, not never, and nobody\nelse notices."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding a larger queue not fix an overloaded service?","options":["Larger queues consume GPU memory that the model needs","Queue depth converts directly into wait time; once wait exceeds what callers tolerate, the server serves requests that have already been abandoned","Queues only work for CPU-bound services, not GPU-bound ones","Larger queues reduce the service rate by increasing lock contention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By Little's law, waiting requests equal arrival rate times wait time. A deeper queue lets wait time grow past client timeouts, so capacity is spent on work nobody will receive."},{"q":"Where should load shedding decisions be made, and why?","options":["After inference, so the server can return a partial answer instead of an error","At the client, because only it knows its own priority","As early and cheaply as possible, before retrieval and prompt assembly, so a rejection preserves capacity instead of spending it","In the database layer, where contention is easiest to measure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A rejection that costs microseconds protects the service. Rejecting after seconds of queueing or after expensive preprocessing has already consumed the resources shedding was meant to save."},{"q":"Which signal is the best trigger for shedding on a GPU-backed LLM endpoint?","options":["CPU utilisation above 80%","Total requests per second exceeding last week's average","Queue wait time of admitted requests approaching the latency budget","Number of open TCP connections"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Queue wait is what users experience and tracks real capacity regardless of model size or prompt length. CPU is irrelevant for GPU-bound work, and request counts ignore how expensive each request is."}]},{"key":"reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (chapter on handling overload)"],"why":"Take a gateway that fronts one provider key rated, illustratively, at one million tokens per minute and\nshared by forty customers. Tenant A starts a nightly job that re-embeds 50,000 documents and submits\nthem as fast as its client can. A global rate limiter keeps the gateway under the provider's limit, so\nnothing errors upstream. But the queue in front of that limiter is first-come-first-served, and it is now\n20,000 deep with A's requests. Tenant B's support chat, which needs 400 tokens and a two-second answer,\nwaits behind all of them. B's p95 triples, B opens a ticket, and your dashboards show the provider\nperfectly healthy.\n\nThe naive answers each fail for a specific reason. \"Add a global rate limit\" protects the upstream, not\nthe neighbors: it bounds the total but says nothing about how the total is divided. \"Scale up\" does not\nhelp when the scarce resource is a vendor quota or a fixed GPU pool you cannot buy more of tonight, and\nit rewards the tenant who misbehaves with everyone's money. \"Ask tenants to behave\" ignores that agents\nloop, clients retry, and a tenant's bug becomes your incident.\n\nThree properties of AI workloads make this sharper than in classic SaaS. Work per request spans three\nor more orders of magnitude (a 20-token classification versus a 100k-token summary), so counting\nrequests measures almost nothing. Agents fan out, so one user action can become fifty calls. And the\nbinding resource often belongs to a vendor, so you cannot engineer your way out by adding capacity; you\ncan only allocate what you already have.","how":"### Choose the units you meter\nFour quantities matter, and they fail in different ways. Tokens per minute (input plus output) track\nprovider limits and cost. Requests per minute catch chatty clients. Concurrent in-flight requests\nprotect whatever is actually saturating: provider concurrency, KV-cache memory, worker slots. Spend per\nday bounds the bill. Meter tokens and concurrency at minimum, because a tenant can stay under any\nrequest count while consuming the whole token budget with large prompts, and because latency collapse\nis a function of concurrency, not of request rate.\n\n### Enforce at admission with a token bucket per tenant\nA token bucket has a capacity (the burst it tolerates) and a refill rate (the sustained allowance). Each\ntenant gets its own. The twist for LLM calls is that output size is unknown until the call finishes, so\nthe gateway reserves the worst case (prompt tokens plus `max_tokens`), makes the call, then reconciles\nby refunding the unused part. It works this way because the alternative, charging after the fact, lets a\ntenant go arbitrarily far over quota inside a single minute. When the bucket cannot cover the\nreservation, reject immediately with 429 and a `Retry-After` header rather than queueing: a queue that\ngrows past the budget just moves the noisy-neighbor problem into the queue, which is exactly the\nopening example.\n\n### Isolate the shared path with lanes and fair scheduling\nQuotas alone are not isolation. Forty tenants each under quota can still sum to more than the provider\nallows, and a FIFO dispatcher will still serve them in arrival order. So the dispatcher that drains to\nthe provider keeps one queue per tenant and services them round-robin, or weighted by tier, which is a\nsimple form of weighted fair queuing. A tenant with 20,000 queued requests then gets one turn per cycle,\nthe same as a tenant with one. Pair this with a per-tenant concurrency semaphore so a single tenant\ncannot hold every in-flight slot. Fair scheduling is work-conserving: when only one tenant is active it\nreceives everything, which hard caps alone can never offer.\n\n### Tier the isolation\nPooled tenants share buckets and lanes. Tenants who pay for guarantees get a dedicated pool: their own\nprovider key or model deployment, their own worker group, their own vector-index replica. This is the\nbulkhead pattern applied per tenant, and it is the only level at which a vendor-side limit (a per-key\nTPM cap) is genuinely isolated, because the vendor's limiter does not know your tenants exist.\n\n### Degrade before you deny\nWhen a tenant reaches its cap, the cheapest useful behavior is often not a 429 but a downgrade: route\nto a smaller model, shed its batch-class traffic while keeping interactive traffic, or lower its\n`max_tokens`. That requires classifying traffic (interactive versus batch, user-initiated versus\nagent-initiated) at the edge so the scheduler can shed the right thing first.\n\n### Make it observable and billable\nTag every request, span and cost record with `tenant_id`. Per-tenant queue wait, in-flight count, tokens\nconsumed and 429 counts are the signals that tell you whether isolation is working, and they settle the\n\"you throttled us\" conversation with data. Quota disputes are a commercial problem, and the only defense\nis a measurement both sides trust.","example":"A single-process sketch of the admission layer: a per-tenant token bucket for tokens per minute with\nreserve-then-reconcile, plus a per-tenant semaphore for concurrency. In production the bucket state\nlives in Redis behind an atomic Lua script so every gateway replica sees the same level; the logic is\nidentical.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass QuotaExceeded(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, tenant, retry_after):\n        super().__init__(f\"tenant {tenant} over token quota\"); self.retry_after = retry_after\n\nclass TenantLimiter:\n    \"\"\"Per-tenant token bucket (tokens/min) + concurrency lane. Single-process version.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, tokens_per_min, max_in_flight):\n        self.rate, self.cap = tokens_per_min / 60.0, float(tokens_per_min)\n        self.level = defaultdict(lambda: self.cap)           # starts full: one burst is allowed\n        self.stamp = defaultdict(time.monotonic)\n        self.lanes = defaultdict(lambda: asyncio.Semaphore(max_in_flight))\n\n    def _refill(self, t):\n        now = time.monotonic()\n        self.level[t] = min(self.cap, self.level[t] + (now - self.stamp[t]) * self.rate)\n        self.stamp[t] = now\n\n    def reserve(self, t, est_tokens):\n        self._refill(t)\n        if self.level[t] < est_tokens:                       # reject now; queueing would hide overload\n            raise QuotaExceeded(t, retry_after=(est_tokens - self.level[t]) / self.rate)\n        self.level[t] -= est_tokens\n\n    def reconcile(self, t, est_tokens, used_tokens):\n        self.level[t] += est_tokens - used_tokens            # refund the unused reservation\n\nasync def call_llm(limiter, tenant, prompt_tokens, max_output_tokens, call):\n    est = prompt_tokens + max_output_tokens                  # worst case: output size unknown until done\n    limiter.reserve(tenant, est)\n    async with limiter.lanes[tenant]:                        # the tenant's own concurrency lane\n        try:\n            resp = await call()\n        except Exception:\n            limiter.reconcile(tenant, est, prompt_tokens)    # failed calls still charge the prompt\n            raise\n    limiter.reconcile(tenant, est, resp[\"usage\"][\"total_tokens\"])\n    return resp\n```\n\nNotice the ordering: the reservation happens before the lane is taken and before any provider call, so\na tenant over quota never occupies a slot. A failed call still charges the prompt tokens, so a retry\nstorm counts against the tenant that caused it rather than against the shared pool. The 429 carries a\n`retry_after` computed from the refill rate, which gives well-behaved clients an honest wait instead of\na guess.","tradeoffs":["Hard per-tenant caps guarantee isolation but strand capacity: if the caps sum to the provider limit, each tenant gets 1/N even when the others are idle. This flips toward soft caps with fair-share scheduling when tenants are many and bursts are uncorrelated, and back toward hard caps when you have sold specific capacity to specific customers.","Dedicated pools and provider keys per tenant give the strongest isolation, including against vendor limits, but carry per-tenant minimum cost and operational surface. Worth it when a tenant's revenue or contractual commitments exceed the cost of the idle capacity.","Metering tokens is accurate but requires reserve-then-reconcile and a tokenizer at admission; metering requests is nearly free but gameable with large prompts. Request counting is adequate only when a schema bounds prompt size.","Rejecting at the cap pushes work back to the client's retry logic and keeps latency honest; queueing instead smooths bursts but hides overload and raises everyone's latency. Queue only batch traffic, and only with a bounded depth."],"pitfalls":["Relying on a single global rate limit. The symptom: the provider never returns 429, yet small tenants' p99 spikes precisely while one tenant runs a batch job.","Counting requests instead of tokens. The symptom: one tenant with 100k-token prompts stays inside its request quota while the cost report attributes most of the monthly spend to a few percent of calls.","Keeping limiter state per replica. The symptom: effective quota equals cap times replica count and silently grows on scale-out; provider 429s appear right after autoscaling events.","Attributing retries to the pool rather than the tenant. The symptom: a tenant's retry storm multiplies upstream load and every other tenant pays for it.","Identifying tenants by API key alone when a customer has many end users. The symptom: the customer complains that \"our app is slow for some users\", because one of their users is starving the rest inside a single quota.","Not reserving output tokens. The symptom: your bucket says the tenant is under quota while the provider rejects for exceeding tokens-per-minute, because long completions were never counted until they finished."],"when":"Reach for this the moment more than one tenant shares a provider key, GPU pool or worker fleet, or when\na single tenant mixes workload classes (batch backfills next to interactive chat). Start small: a Redis\ntoken bucket keyed by tenant for tokens per minute plus a per-tenant concurrency semaphore covers most\nearly-stage services. Add per-tenant queues with fair draining when batch jobs appear, and dedicated\npools when a contract promises capacity. Measure per-tenant queue wait, share of tokens by tenant, and\n429s split by tenant and by upstream; isolation is working when one tenant's spike moves only that\ntenant's lines. In interviews the prompt is usually \"a customer's batch job slowed everyone down; what\ndo you change?\", and the expected answer distinguishes bounding the total from dividing it fairly.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the shared token bucket and per-tenant semaphore in the gateway, reserves worst-case tokens before each call, and tags every span and cost record with the tenant.","architect":"Owns the tiering model: which units are quota'd, how quotas map to pricing plans and to vendor limits, and which tenants justify dedicated keys, deployments or worker pools.","fde":"Inside a customer's deployment the tenants are departments or business units; the FDE negotiates their quotas, explains the 429s, and builds the per-team usage dashboard that ends disputes."},"check":{"q":"A shared gateway fronts one provider key with an illustrative limit of one million tokens per\nminute for forty tenants. Tenant A starts an embedding backfill and tenant B's chat latency triples, but\nthe global rate limiter never fires. Explain why, and design the fix.","a":"The global limiter bounds total upstream load, not its division; the FIFO queue ahead of it is\ndominated by A, so B waits behind thousands of A's requests while the provider stays healthy. The fix\nhas three layers. First, per-tenant admission: a token bucket per tenant (say an illustrative 50k tokens\nper minute default) with worst-case reservation and a concurrency semaphore, so A is rejected with 429\nand `Retry-After` once over its share. Second, isolation on the shared path: one queue per tenant drained\nround-robin, so even under quota A gets one turn per cycle. Third, classify A's traffic as batch so it\nis shed first and only consumes leftover capacity, and move premium tenants to a dedicated key. Verify\nwith per-tenant queue-wait metrics: after the change, A's wait rises and B's is flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a single global rate limit fail to prevent noisy-neighbor problems?","options":["Global limits only count requests, never tokens","It bounds total upstream load but says nothing about how that load is divided among tenants","Rate limiters cannot run in a distributed gateway","Providers ignore client-side limits and apply their own"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A global limit protects the dependency, so the provider looks healthy, but the queue in front of it is still served in arrival order; one tenant's burst fills it and everyone else waits behind. Isolation needs per-tenant admission and fair scheduling, not a tighter total."},{"q":"Why should an LLM gateway meter tokens rather than only requests per minute?","options":["Token counts are easier to compute than request counts","Providers do not enforce request-rate limits","Cost and provider limits scale with tokens, so one large-prompt tenant can exhaust the budget while under its request quota","Request counting cannot be stored in Redis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Work per request varies by orders of magnitude; a tenant sending 100k-token prompts at a modest request rate consumes the tokens-per-minute budget that other tenants need. Requests are a useful secondary signal, not the primary unit."},{"q":"Which mechanism keeps isolation work-conserving, so idle capacity is not stranded?","options":["Hard per-tenant caps that sum to exactly the provider limit","Per-replica limiters with no shared state","Fair-share scheduling across per-tenant queues with soft caps","A dedicated provider key for every tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Round-robin or weighted draining of per-tenant queues gives each active tenant an equal turn while a lone active tenant receives the whole pool. Hard 1/N caps leave capacity unused when others are idle, and per-replica limiters break isolation on scale-out."}]},{"key":"reliability/circuit-breakers","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Fowler, 2014 — CircuitBreaker (bliki)","Official docs: Resilience4j"],"why":"Cascading failure has a precise mechanism. Suppose an agent service calls a provider with a 30-second\ntimeout and handles 50 requests per second. The provider degrades to 100 percent timeouts. Each\nin-flight call now holds a worker and a connection for the full 30 seconds, so by Little's law the\nservice accumulates 50 × 30 = 1,500 stuck calls, far more than any sensible pool. Within a minute the\npool is exhausted and the service cannot answer requests that never needed the provider at all: health\nchecks fail, the orchestrator restarts pods, and a partial outage upstream becomes a total outage here.\nMeanwhile every client retry doubles the load on a dependency that is struggling precisely because it is\noverloaded.\n\nThe obvious mitigations are each insufficient on their own. Timeouts bound a single call but not the\naggregate; 1,500 stuck calls at 30 seconds each is the result *with* timeouts. Retries with backoff make\nthe aggregate worse during an outage, because they multiply attempts exactly when attempts are\nworthless. Waiting for the provider to fix itself is not a strategy when your own service dies first.\n\nThe breaker is the only one of these that does two things at once: it stops consuming the caller's\nresources immediately, because rejected calls return in microseconds rather than seconds, and it\nremoves load from the dependency so that whatever is wrong there has a chance to clear. The name comes\nfrom electrical breakers, which exist for the same reason: when a fault draws too much current, the\nright response is to open the circuit, not to keep supplying power and hope.","how":"### Three states\nA breaker is a small state machine wrapped around a call.\n\n1. **Closed.** Calls flow through. Every outcome is recorded in a sliding window, either the last N calls\n   or the last T seconds. If the failure ratio in the window crosses a threshold *and* the window holds\n   at least a minimum number of calls, the breaker trips. The minimum exists because one failure out of\n   one call is a 100 percent failure rate and should not trip anything.\n2. **Open.** Every call is rejected immediately with a distinct error (`CircuitOpen`), no network\n   involved, for a reset interval. This is where resources are freed and the dependency gets relief.\n3. **Half-open.** When the interval elapses, a limited number of trial calls are allowed. If they succeed\n   the breaker closes and the window is cleared; if any fails it reopens and the timer restarts. The\n   limit matters because if every waiting caller rushed in on the first tick of recovery, a dependency\n   that is only partly healed would be knocked down again.\n\n### What counts as a failure\nTimeouts, connection errors and 5xx responses are failures: they describe the dependency's health. A\n429 is usually treated as a failure for tripping purposes because continuing to call a rate-limited\nendpoint helps nobody, though its `Retry-After` should also inform the reset interval. 4xx client errors\nsuch as a malformed request or an invalid schema are *not* failures of the dependency; they are your bug,\nand counting them opens the breaker on a healthy provider. For LLM traffic add one more category: slow\nsuccess. A provider answering correctly in 25 seconds is, for an interactive product, down. Libraries\nsuch as Resilience4j expose a slow-call-rate threshold for exactly this; if yours does not, a tight\ntimeout turns slowness into a countable failure.\n\n### Choosing thresholds\nA workable starting point is a window of 50 to 100 calls, a minimum of 20, trip at 50 percent failures,\nreset after 30 seconds, and 3 half-open probes. The reasoning: the window should be large enough that a\nsingle burst of errors does not trip it, the minimum large enough to be statistically meaningful at your\ntraffic level, the ratio high enough that ordinary error rates (a few percent) never approach it, and\nthe reset interval short enough that recovery is noticed within a minute. Low-traffic dependencies need\nlonger time-based windows; at two calls a minute a 20-call minimum would take ten minutes to trip.\n\n### Granularity\nOne breaker per failure domain, not one per service name. A provider's `model-a` deployment in one\nregion can be failing while `model-b` is fine, so key the breaker by provider × model × region, and tool\nAPIs by endpoint. A single \"the provider\" breaker either trips on partial failures and takes healthy\npaths with it, or never trips because healthy traffic dilutes the ratio.\n\n### What happens when it is open\nA breaker without a fallback converts slow failures into fast failures. That is already worth having,\nsince it keeps the service and its health checks alive, but the user experience is rescued by what you do\nwith `CircuitOpen`: route to a secondary provider or smaller model, return a cached or degraded answer,\nor enqueue the work for later and say so. The breaker is the detector; the fallback is the recovery.\n\n### Composition with retries, timeouts and bulkheads\nA common arrangement, and the one Resilience4j documents as its default, puts retry outermost, then the\nbreaker, then the timeout around the actual call. Each retry attempt is then counted by the breaker, and\nonce the breaker opens the remaining attempts short-circuit instantly, provided the retry policy treats\n`CircuitOpen` as non-retryable. Put the retry *inside* the breaker and each counted \"call\" takes several\ntimeouts to register, delaying the trip while multiplying load. A bulkhead (a concurrency cap per\ndependency) sits alongside: it bounds how many callers can be stuck while the breaker is still\naccumulating its minimum calls.","example":"A compact asyncio breaker with a count-based window, a minimum-calls guard, a capped number of half-open\nprobes, and a fallback at the call site. It is single-process; a fleet shares breaker state through a\nlibrary or a small Redis record, but the state machine is the same.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, collections, time\n\nclass CircuitOpen(Exception): pass\nclass UpstreamError(Exception): pass          # 5xx / connection-level failures from the provider\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    def __init__(self, window=50, min_calls=20, fail_ratio=0.5, reset_after=30.0, probes=3):\n        self.window, self.min_calls, self.fail_ratio = window, min_calls, fail_ratio\n        self.reset_after, self.probes = reset_after, probes\n        self.results = collections.deque(maxlen=window)    # True = failed call\n        self.state, self.opened_at, self.probe_ok, self.probe_inflight = \"closed\", 0.0, 0, 0\n\n    def _allow(self):\n        if self.state == \"open\":\n            if time.monotonic() - self.opened_at < self.reset_after:\n                return False                                # fail fast: no network call at all\n            self.state, self.probe_ok, self.probe_inflight = \"half_open\", 0, 0\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":\n            if self.probe_inflight >= self.probes:\n                return False                                # only a few trial calls at a time\n            self.probe_inflight += 1\n        return True\n\n    def _trip(self):\n        self.state, self.opened_at = \"open\", time.monotonic()\n\n    def _record(self, failed):\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":\n            self.probe_inflight -= 1\n            if failed: self._trip(); return                # still broken: reopen, timer restarts\n            self.probe_ok += 1\n            if self.probe_ok >= self.probes:                # enough evidence of recovery\n                self.state, self.results = \"closed\", collections.deque(maxlen=self.window)\n            return\n        self.results.append(failed)\n        n = len(self.results)\n        if n >= self.min_calls and sum(self.results) / n >= self.fail_ratio:\n            self._trip()\n\n    async def call(self, fn, *args, timeout=10.0, **kw):\n        if not self._allow():\n            raise CircuitOpen(\"dependency marked unhealthy\")\n        try:\n            result = await asyncio.wait_for(fn(*args, **kw), timeout)\n        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, ConnectionError, UpstreamError):\n            self._record(True); raise                       # 4xx client errors are deliberately not counted\n        self._record(False)\n        return result\n\nbreaker = CircuitBreaker()\n\nasync def answer(prompt):\n    try:\n        return await breaker.call(primary_llm, prompt, timeout=20)\n    except CircuitOpen:\n        return await secondary_llm(prompt)                   # other vendor, smaller model, or cached reply\n```\n\nThe timeout lives inside `call` so that slow responses become failures the window can see. Emit a metric\non every state transition; a breaker that opens silently is an outage nobody is looking at.","tradeoffs":["Aggressive thresholds protect the caller quickly but trip on transient blips and reject requests that would have succeeded; the balance shifts toward longer windows and higher minimums as traffic per breaker falls.","Fine-grained breakers (per model, per region, per endpoint) isolate precisely, but each partition may never reach its minimum calls; coarse breakers trip reliably yet take healthy sub-dependencies down with the sick one. Partition only as finely as your traffic supports.","A short reset interval notices recovery fast but re-hammers a dependency that is still down; a long one keeps you on the fallback longer than needed. The better your fallback, the longer you can afford to wait.","Local per-process breakers need no coordination, but every replica discovers the outage independently and small replicas may never accumulate enough calls; shared state fixes that at the cost of a network hop and a new dependency on the store."],"pitfalls":["Counting 4xx client errors as failures. The symptom: the breaker opens after you ship a bad prompt template or schema change while the provider's status page is green, and the fallback quietly serves degraded answers.","Omitting the minimum-calls guard. The symptom: open/closed flapping in the state metric overnight, when traffic is low and a single failure is a 100 percent rate.","Retrying across an open breaker. The symptom: latency does not drop when the breaker opens because the retry policy keeps re-raising `CircuitOpen` with backoff, and logs fill with rejections.","Ignoring slow success. The symptom: worker and connection pools exhaust during a latency incident while the breaker stays closed because nothing returned an error.","Opening without alerting or fallback. The symptom: a sudden wall of fast 503s that nobody connects to an upstream problem, because the service's own error rate looks like the cause.","Letting every replica probe at the same instant. The symptom: a dependency that recovers for ten seconds and collapses again on each reset tick; add jitter to the reset interval."],"when":"Wrap any synchronous call to a dependency that can fail slowly and whose failure you can do something\nabout: provider and model endpoints, tool and partner APIs, vector stores, anything over a network. Do\nnot bother for in-process calls, and think twice when there is no fallback and the request genuinely\nmust wait; there, a tight timeout plus a bounded queue is more honest than a breaker that rejects work\nyou will have to redo. Measure state transitions, rejected-call counts, time spent open, and fallback\nrate, and alert when a breaker has been open longer than its reset interval several times running. In\ninterviews the expected depth is the three states, why half-open exists, what should and should not count\nas a failure, and how the breaker composes with timeouts, retries and bulkheads.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps every provider, model and tool client in breaker plus timeout plus fallback, keys breakers by failure domain, and emits state-transition metrics.","architect":"Defines the failure domains that get their own breaker, the fallback policy for each dependency, and demands the cascading-failure walk-through in design reviews.","fde":"In a customer's environment the flaky dependencies are their own systems (CRM, ticketing, identity); the breaker keeps the agent responsive and the FDE explains why it said \"tool unavailable\" rather than hanging."},"check":{"q":"Your agent calls a customer's ticketing API. At their peak hour the API's p99 climbs to 40 seconds\nwithout returning errors, your breaker (set to open at 50 percent failures) never opens, and your\nservice's worker pool exhausts. What is wrong, and how do you fix it?","a":"The breaker only sees errors, and the failure mode here is slow success, so from its point of view\nnothing is failing. Fix it in layers. Put a timeout appropriate to the product, perhaps 5 seconds, inside\nthe breaker so slow calls become countable failures, or enable a slow-call-rate threshold if the library\nhas one. Add a bulkhead that caps concurrent calls to that API, so even before the breaker trips the\nstuck calls cannot take more than, say, 20 of 200 workers. Check the minimum-calls setting against this\nAPI's traffic so the trip is reachable. Then give `CircuitOpen` a fallback: queue the ticket write for\nasynchronous retry and tell the user it has been queued, rather than failing the whole turn. Finally,\nverify with metrics: the breaker should now open within a minute of the latency spike and worker-pool\nutilization should stay flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the purpose of the half-open state?","options":["To let all queued requests through at once when the reset interval ends","To test recovery with a limited number of trial calls without flooding a dependency that may still be unhealthy","To count client errors separately from server errors","To hold requests in memory until the dependency recovers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If every waiting caller rushed in at the first sign of recovery, a partly healed dependency would be knocked down again. A few probes give evidence of recovery cheaply; one failure reopens the breaker."},{"q":"Which outcome should NOT count toward tripping a breaker on an LLM provider call?","options":["A request that exceeded its timeout","A 503 Service Unavailable response","A 400 Bad Request caused by a malformed payload from your own code","A connection refused error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The breaker measures the dependency's health. A 400 from your own bad request says nothing about the provider, and counting it would open the circuit, and switch to a fallback, while the provider is fine."},{"q":"A team places a 3-attempt retry policy inside the breaker (breaker outermost, retries inside). During an outage, what is the effect?","options":["Nothing changes; the order of resilience wrappers is irrelevant","The breaker trips faster because each attempt is counted","Each counted call takes roughly three timeouts to register as a failure, delaying the trip while retries triple load on the failing dependency","The retries are skipped automatically once the first attempt fails"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With retries inside, the breaker sees one slow call per three real attempts, so it accumulates failures three times more slowly and the dependency receives three times the traffic. Put retry outside the breaker and make `CircuitOpen` non-retryable."}]},{"key":"reliability/bulkheads","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Official docs: Resilience4j"],"why":"Picture a service with one pool of 200 workers that calls an LLM provider, a vector database and a\ncustomer's CRM API. The CRM API hangs: connections open, no bytes return, each call lasts until the\n30-second timeout. Every request that touches the CRM now holds a worker for 30 seconds. At only 5 CRM\ncalls per second, Little's law (concurrency = arrival rate × time in system) says 5 × 30 = 150 workers are\nstuck in steady state, three quarters of the pool, reached within half a minute. Add normal LLM traffic\nand the pool is full. Requests that need only the LLM and the vector store, both perfectly healthy, are\nnow rejected or queued. Health checks share the pool too, so the orchestrator restarts the pods. One slow\ndependency became a complete outage.\n\nThe usual first responses do not solve this. A bigger pool delays the moment of exhaustion by the ratio\nyou grew it. Timeouts bound each call, but the arithmetic above already includes the timeout; aggregate\noccupancy is rate × timeout, and a shorter timeout only shrinks the number, it does not cap it. A circuit\nbreaker helps once it trips, but it needs a minimum number of observed failures first, and a hung\ndependency that never returns an error can take minutes to register. None of these stops the healthy\npaths from being starved in the meantime.\n\nThe same shape appears with workload mixing rather than failure: a batch summarization job that is\nmerely slow and plentiful fills the pool and interactive users wait behind it. Nothing is broken, and\nthe service is still unusable. The missing property is a hard ceiling per compartment, which is what a\nbulkhead supplies.","how":"### Size with Little's law\nThe concurrency a dependency can consume is its call rate times its latency, and in the failure case\nlatency equals the timeout. The bulkhead cap for a dependency is therefore its normal rate × normal\nlatency, with headroom for bursts, and crucially it must be a small fraction of the total pool. The\nformula tells you two things: how large a cap is safe, and how much damage the dependency can do at\nworst, because occupancy can never exceed the cap no matter how slow it gets.\n\n### Five forms, from cheapest to strongest\n1. **Semaphore bulkhead.** A counting semaphore per dependency in the process. Acquire before the call,\n   release after; if no permit is free, fail immediately or wait for a very short bounded time. Nearly\n   free, and sufficient when the client is non-blocking. This is the default choice in async services.\n2. **Thread-pool bulkhead.** A separate executor per dependency. It isolates blocking clients and\n   CPU-bound work (a synchronous SDK, a tokenizer, PDF parsing) so they cannot stall the event loop or\n   each other. It costs threads and context switches, which is why it is reserved for blocking work.\n3. **Connection-pool bulkhead.** A separate HTTP client, with its own connection limits, per upstream.\n   This is needed *in addition to* a semaphore because the connection pool is itself a shared resource:\n   a hung upstream holds its connections open, and if every dependency shares one client, the healthy\n   ones cannot get a socket even though their semaphores show headroom.\n4. **Queue bulkhead.** Separate queues and consumer groups per workload class (interactive versus batch,\n   per tenant tier). The consumer fleet is the compartment, so a flood of batch jobs can fill the batch\n   queue to any depth without delaying an interactive message.\n5. **Deployment bulkhead.** Separate pods, node pools, namespaces with resource quotas, or clusters per\n   workload class or tenant tier, and separate provider keys or model deployments. Strongest isolation,\n   highest cost, and the only form that also isolates vendor-side limits.\n\n### What to compartment by\nBy dependency is the most common and should be the baseline. By workload class protects interactive\nlatency from batch volume. By tenant tier is how you sell isolation. By criticality is the one teams\nforget: health and readiness endpoints, authentication and the admission layer must never share a\ncompartment with LLM calls, or an upstream outage turns into restarts and lock-outs.\n\n### Reject or wait\nWhen a compartment is full, the caller either fails fast or waits a bounded, short time for a permit.\nUnbounded waiting is forbidden because it recreates the shared-pool problem one level up: the waiters\nare now the stuck resource. A rejection should carry enough information for a graceful degradation\n(answer without the CRM context, serve from cache, enqueue for later) or an honest 503 with\n`Retry-After`.\n\n### How it composes with breakers and load shedding\nA bulkhead is a static limit that bounds damage regardless of health. A breaker is a dynamic reaction\nthat stops sending once failure is observed. Load shedding is global admission control under overload.\nThey stack: the bulkhead keeps a hung dependency from exhausting the pool while the breaker accumulates\nits minimum calls; once the breaker opens, even the bulkhead's permits are freed; and if the whole\nservice is saturated, shedding drops the lowest-value work first.","example":"Per-dependency semaphore bulkheads with a short bounded wait, separate HTTP clients so the connection\npool is partitioned too, and a degrade path when a compartment is full. The caps come from Little's law\nwith illustrative traffic numbers.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, httpx\n\nclass BulkheadFull(Exception): pass\n\nclass Bulkhead:\n    \"\"\"Caps concurrent calls to one dependency; a caller waits at most max_wait for a slot.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, name, max_concurrent, max_wait=0.25):\n        self.name, self.max_wait = name, max_wait\n        self.sem = asyncio.Semaphore(max_concurrent)\n\n    async def run(self, fn, *args, **kw):\n        try:\n            await asyncio.wait_for(self.sem.acquire(), self.max_wait)\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            raise BulkheadFull(f\"{self.name} at capacity\")     # fail fast; never queue without bound\n        try:\n            return await fn(*args, **kw)\n        finally:\n            self.sem.release()                                  # released on success, error or cancellation\n\n# Little's law: concurrency = call rate x latency, plus headroom. Illustrative numbers:\n# LLM 20 rps x 4 s = 80 -> cap 100; vector DB 40 rps x 0.05 s = 2 -> cap 10; CRM 5 rps x 2 s = 10 -> cap 20.\nLLM = Bulkhead(\"llm\", 100)\nVEC = Bulkhead(\"vector_db\", 10)\nCRM = Bulkhead(\"crm_api\", 20, max_wait=0.05)\n\n# Separate HTTP clients: the connection pool is a shared resource as well.\nllm_http = httpx.AsyncClient(limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=100), timeout=30.0)\ncrm_http = httpx.AsyncClient(limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=20), timeout=5.0)\n\nasync def handle(req):\n    docs = await VEC.run(search, req.query)\n    try:\n        account = await CRM.run(lookup_account, req.user_id)\n    except BulkheadFull:\n        account = None                                          # degrade: answer without CRM context\n    return await LLM.run(generate, req, docs, account)\n```\n\nWith the CRM hung, at most 20 permits plus 20 connections are ever tied up; the other 180 workers keep\nserving requests that need only retrieval and the model, and CRM-dependent requests degrade in 50\nmilliseconds instead of hanging for 30 seconds. The `finally` matters: a permit leaked on cancellation\nis a compartment that slowly fills with ghosts.","tradeoffs":["Isolation versus utilization: capacity in one compartment cannot be borrowed by another, so the CRM's 20 permits sit idle when the CRM is quiet. When load is bursty and uncorrelated, soft partitions (a guaranteed minimum per compartment plus a shared overflow pool) recover most of the waste.","Semaphores are cheap but only bound non-blocking work; thread pools isolate blocking and CPU-bound calls at the cost of threads; separate deployments isolate everything, including vendor limits, at the cost of money. Escalate only when the cheaper form demonstrably leaks.","A tiny `max_wait` fails fast but rejects during ordinary micro-bursts; a longer one absorbs bursts but ties callers up. Interactive paths want the short wait; batch paths can afford to queue, in their own queue.","Many fine compartments give precise isolation and precise metrics but multiply configuration and the chance of mis-sizing one; coarse compartments are simple but share blast radius. Partition by failure domain, not by code module."],"pitfalls":["Setting the cap as a large fraction of the pool. The symptom: a bulkhead of 150 out of 200 \"works\" until the dependency hangs, and the service still cascades because 150 stuck workers is an outage.","Sharing one HTTP client across dependencies behind separate semaphores. The symptom: the CRM semaphore shows five in flight while CRM calls hang, because the LLM's slow responses hold every connection in the common pool.","Blocking calls inside async code. The symptom: every compartment reports headroom, yet everything including health checks stalls, because a synchronous SDK call froze the event loop that all the semaphores live on.","Letting health and readiness endpoints share the worker pool. The symptom: during a dependency outage the orchestrator restarts healthy pods, converting a partial outage into a total one.","No degrade path for `BulkheadFull`. The symptom: user-visible error rate rises in lockstep with the share of requests that happen to touch the saturated dependency, even though the answer could have been given without it.","Releasing the permit only on the success path. The symptom: capacity drifts downward over days; the compartment reads full with no real work in flight, and a restart \"fixes\" it."],"when":"Introduce bulkheads as soon as a service talks to more than one dependency with different latency or\nreliability, or serves more than one workload class. The baseline is cheap: a semaphore per dependency\nsized from Little's law, a separate HTTP client per upstream, and a dedicated path for health checks.\nPromote to queue-level separation when batch and interactive traffic share a fleet, and to deployment\nseparation when a tenant pays for it or a vendor key limit must be isolated. Measure per-compartment\nin-flight count, rejection rate and wait time; alert on sustained saturation of any one compartment,\nwhich is an early warning long before the breaker trips. In interviews the question is \"one slow\ndependency took down the whole service, how do you prevent it\", and the complete answer is timeouts to\nbound each call, bulkheads to bound the aggregate, and breakers to stop sending, with the sizing\narithmetic to show you understand why each is needed.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures per-dependency semaphores and per-upstream clients, moves blocking SDKs onto their own executor, and writes the degrade path for every `BulkheadFull`.","architect":"Decides the compartment boundaries, by dependency, workload class, tenant tier and criticality, and whether each lives in-process, at the queue, or as a separate deployment.","fde":"Uses bulkheads so a customer's flaky legacy system cannot take down the agent, and separates the customer's bulk backfills from their users' interactive sessions inside one deployment."},"check":{"q":"An agent service runs one shared pool of 200 workers with 30-second timeouts. It calls an LLM\n(about 20 requests per second, 3 seconds each) and a CRM API (5 requests per second, normally 300 ms).\nThe CRM API hangs. Walk through what happens with numbers, then show how bulkheads change the outcome.","a":"With the CRM hung, each CRM call occupies a worker until the 30-second timeout. Little's law gives\n5 × 30 = 150 workers stuck in steady state, reached within about 30 seconds. LLM traffic needs 20 × 3 = 60\nworkers, so total demand is 210 against a pool of 200: the pool saturates, LLM-only requests queue or\nfail, health checks miss, and pods restart. With a CRM bulkhead capped at 20 permits and a 50 ms maximum\nwait, CRM occupancy can never exceed 20; 180 workers remain, more than the 60 the LLM path needs.\nCRM-dependent requests receive `BulkheadFull` within 50 ms and degrade by answering without CRM context.\nA separate HTTP client for the CRM keeps its hung sockets out of the LLM's connection pool. Shrinking the\nCRM timeout to 5 seconds additionally lowers worst-case demand to 25 even without the cap, and a circuit\nbreaker on top releases the 20 permits once failures are confirmed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the essential difference between a bulkhead and a circuit breaker?","options":["A bulkhead retries failed calls; a breaker does not","A bulkhead statically bounds resource use per compartment regardless of health; a breaker dynamically stops calls after observing failures","A bulkhead applies to databases and a breaker to HTTP APIs","They are two names for the same mechanism in different libraries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bulkhead works before and without detection: a hung dependency can never hold more than its cap. The breaker needs evidence of failure first, then removes load entirely. They are complementary, not interchangeable."},{"q":"A dependency is called 10 times per second with a 20-second timeout. In the worst case, how many concurrent calls can it tie up without a bulkhead?","options":["10","20","200","2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Little's law: concurrency equals arrival rate times time in system, and when the dependency hangs the time in system is the timeout, so 10 × 20 = 200. The bulkhead cap replaces that number with a ceiling you choose."},{"q":"Why give each upstream its own HTTP client even when per-dependency semaphores are already in place?","options":["Separate clients make requests faster by avoiding TLS handshakes","The connection pool is itself a shared resource, and a hung upstream's open connections can exhaust it for healthy upstreams","Semaphores do not work with HTTP clients","It is required by the HTTP/2 specification"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A semaphore bounds how many calls your code starts, but a shared client has its own connection limit; sockets held open by a hung dependency are unavailable to the others, so the healthy paths stall while their semaphores show plenty of permits."}]},{"key":"reliability/fmea","sources":["AIAG & VDA, 2019 — FMEA Handbook"],"why":"Most teams discover failure modes one incident at a time. Postmortems are reactive by definition, and\nchaos experiments only test hypotheses you already hold. Something has to generate the hypotheses, and\n\"we will add monitoring and handle issues as they come\" generates them at the worst possible moment.\n\nThis is especially weak for AI systems because their worst failures are silent. The service returns\n200 OK with a plausible, wrong answer. The embedding index stops refreshing and retrieval drifts stale\nover weeks. An agent's tool call succeeds, against the wrong customer's record. Availability monitoring\nsees a healthy system throughout. Intuition does not help either: engineers rank failures by vividness,\nso \"the provider goes down\" gets a failover project while \"the tenant filter is missing on the new\nindex\" gets nothing, even though the second is catastrophic and the first is a nuisance with a retry.\n\nFMEA counters both problems mechanically. Walking the request path component by component with a\nchecklist of ways to fail forces attention onto boring, high-consequence modes. Scoring detection\nseparately from likelihood and severity surfaces the key asymmetry: a failure you cannot see is more\ndangerous than one that pages you, because its effect compounds until a customer reports it. The output\nis not a document but a ranked backlog: the top rows become guards, evals, alerts and chaos experiments,\nin that order of priority.","how":"### Decompose the system into functions\nList the steps in the request path and the cross-cutting concerns. For a retrieval-augmented agent:\ningestion, chunking, embedding, index refresh, query embedding, retrieval, tenant scoping, prompt\nassembly, model call, output parsing and validation, tool execution, response delivery; plus\nauthentication, rate limiting, logging and the deployment process itself. Granularity should match the\ndecisions you can act on; \"the backend\" is too coarse, individual functions in a file too fine.\n\n### Enumerate failure modes per function\nA failure mode is *how* the function fails, stated as an observable deviation, not as \"it breaks\". Use\nguide words to force breadth, borrowed from hazard analysis: none (no output), more, less, late, early,\nwrong, partial, stale, duplicated, to the wrong recipient, without authorization. Applied to retrieval\nthis yields \"returns nothing\", \"returns too many chunks and truncates the prompt\", \"returns stale\ndocuments\", \"returns another tenant's documents\", \"returns the right documents too slowly\". A team that\nonly lists \"retrieval is down\" has not done the step.\n\n### Effects and Severity (S)\nDescribe the effect at the user or business level, then score 1 to 10. A usable scale for software:\n9 to 10 for irreversible or legal harm (money moved, data leaked, unsafe advice acted on), 6 to 8 for a\nwrong result the user is likely to act on, 3 to 5 for visible degradation or a failed request, 1 to 2\nfor cosmetic issues. Anchor each number with a written example so two raters agree.\n\n### Causes and Occurrence (O)\nFor each mode list the mechanisms that produce it, and score how often, 1 (practically never) to 10\n(constant). Use data where it exists: provider error rates, eval failure rates, how often the index job\nhas failed historically. Guesses are allowed but must be marked as guesses, because O is the number\nmost often wrong.\n\n### Current controls and Detection (D)\nWhat exists today that would catch the failure *before* its effect reaches the user, and how likely it\nis to do so. Scale 1 (certain: a type check fails the build) to 10 (no way to detect). Note the\ninversion: a high D is bad. \"We have logs\" scores 8 or 9 unless someone is alerted by them, because\nlogs nobody reads detect nothing.\n\n### Risk Priority Number and its limits\nThe classic ranking is RPN = S × O × D, from 1 to 1,000, sorted descending. It is useful because it is\nsimple, and misleading in one specific way: it treats the three dimensions as interchangeable, so a rare,\nundetectable catastrophe (S 10, O 1, D 1 gives 10) can rank below a frequent cosmetic bug (S 2, O 5,\nD 5 gives 50). The automotive industry's newer handbook replaced RPN with an Action Priority table that\nconsiders severity first for this reason. The practical rule: any row with severity 9 or 10 goes to the\ntop regardless of RPN, then sort the rest by RPN.\n\n### Actions, owners and re-scoring\nEach top row gets an action that reduces one of the three factors. Prevention lowers O (a schema that\nmakes the bad input impossible). Containment lowers S (a confirmation step before an irreversible tool,\na spending cap). Detection lowers D (an eval in CI, a freshness alert, a canary query). Assign an owner,\nre-score after the action, and revisit the table on every architectural change. A score that never\nchanges is a sign the table has become decoration.\n\n### Design versus process FMEA\nDesign FMEA analyzes the system as built. Process FMEA analyzes how it is operated and changed: deploys,\nprompt edits, model version upgrades, index rebuilds, credential rotation. AI systems need both, and the\nprocess rows are often the highest: a large share of AI incidents trace to someone changing a prompt or\na model version without an eval gate, not to code.","example":"A fragment of an FMEA for a support assistant that answers from a document index and can issue refunds\nthrough a tool. Scores are the team's judgment calls on a 1 to 10 scale; the point is the ranking, not\nthe precision.\n\n| Function | Failure mode | Effect | S | Cause | O | Current detection | D | RPN |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Index refresh | Serves stale policy docs | Agent quotes outdated refund terms; customer promised the wrong amount | 7 | Nightly rebuild job fails silently | 4 | Job logs only, no alert | 9 | 252 |\n| Refund tool | Refund issued against the wrong order | Money moved irreversibly | 10 | Model copies a similar-looking order id from context | 3 | None before execution | 8 | 240 |\n| Tenant scoping | Retrieval returns another tenant's documents | Data leak across customers | 10 | Filter omitted in a new retrieval client | 2 | None | 9 | 180 |\n| Model call | Provider timeout or 5xx | Request fails; user sees an error and retries | 4 | Provider incident | 5 | Error-rate alert pages on-call | 2 | 40 |\n| Output parsing | Response violates JSON schema | Reply dropped, user sees generic error | 3 | Prompt edit or model drift | 4 | Parser raises; counted and alerted | 3 | 36 |\n\nThe provider outage, the failure everyone had been planning around, ranks last: it is loud, so it is\nalready detected and handled. The three rows that matter are quiet. The actions follow directly. For\nstale documents, add a freshness SLI (age of newest indexed document) with an alert: D drops from 9 to\n3 and the RPN to 84. For the refund tool, require the order id to be passed from the ticket's structured\nfield rather than generated from free text, and add a confirmation step above a value threshold: O\ndrops to 1, D to 2, and the RPN to 20, while S stays 10 because the harm, if it happens, is unchanged.\nFor tenant scoping, make the tenant filter mandatory in the retrieval client's constructor and run an\nautomated cross-tenant canary query hourly: D drops to 2 and the row, still severity 10, is now\ncontained. The team spent one afternoon and re-prioritized a quarter.","tradeoffs":["Breadth versus time: a full FMEA across every component takes days, while a two-hour session over the request path finds most of the high-risk rows. Go deep when the system takes irreversible actions or carries regulatory exposure; stay shallow for an internal tool with a human in the loop.","Numeric scores look objective but encode judgment; consistency between raters matters more than precision. With a small team, anchored scales or even high/medium/low buckets beat spurious decimals.","RPN gives one sortable number but hides rare catastrophes; severity-first ordering protects against catastrophe but can leave frequent, irritating failures unaddressed. Use severity gates first, RPN second.","A living table stays useful across architecture changes but needs an owner and a review cadence; a one-off exercise is cheap but stale after the first major change, and a stale FMEA creates false confidence."],"pitfalls":["Listing only \"component unavailable\" modes. The symptom: the table reads like an uptime checklist and has no rows for wrong, stale, partial or cross-tenant output, which is where AI systems actually fail.","Scoring detection as good because \"we log it\". The symptom: incident reviews that find the signal was sitting in logs for days before a customer reported the problem.","Running the analysis alone. The symptom: the modes that bite live in areas the author does not own, such as the data pipeline, the vendor's change process or the customer's upstream system.","Treating it as a compliance artifact. The symptom: RPNs never re-scored, the action column empty, and the file last modified the week before an audit.","Scoring occurrence from intuition when data exists. The symptom: provider 429s scored as rare while the dashboard shows them daily, so the row is deprioritized and the retry budget never gets fixed.","Confusing cause with mode. The symptom: rows like \"engineer forgot the filter\", which cannot be detected or scored for effect; the mode is \"retrieval returns another tenant's data\", the forgotten filter is one of its causes."],"when":"Run an FMEA before launching anything with tool side effects or external users, before adding any\nirreversible tool to an agent, after a serious incident to find its siblings, and whenever you need to\ndecide what to monitor or what to chaos-test: the D column is a monitoring backlog and the top rows are\nyour first experiments. Skip it for throwaway prototypes, and never let it substitute for testing; it\ntells you where to test. In day-to-day work it shows up as a two-hour workshop with a spreadsheet and\nthe people who own each stage. In interviews the question is \"how would you figure out what could go\nwrong with this agent\"; walk the request path with guide words, score a few rows aloud, and name the\ntop three with their mitigations.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enumerates failure modes for the stages they own, scores them with data from dashboards and evals, and turns the top rows into guards, regression evals and alerts.","architect":"Owns the system-level table across teams and vendors, sets the severity scale, and decides what severity-10 rows require by policy: confirmation steps, spending caps, kill switches.","fde":"Runs a joint FMEA with the customer before go-live, because the customer knows the business effects and their legacy systems' failure modes; the result becomes the hypercare monitoring plan."},"check":{"q":"You are prioritizing reliability work for an agent that reads a customer's CRM and drafts emails\nthat a human approves before sending. One engineer wants to spend the sprint on multi-provider failover.\nUsing FMEA reasoning, how do you evaluate that proposal?","a":"Score the row it addresses. Provider outage: severity about 4, since a human is waiting and can\nretry and nothing irreversible happens; occurrence from the provider's history, perhaps 4; detection 2,\nbecause error-rate alerts already fire. RPN around 32. Now score the quiet rows. \"Agent reads the wrong\ncustomer's record and drafts an email containing their data\": severity 9 if the approver misses it\n(personal data sent to the wrong recipient), occurrence 3 given lookups by fuzzy name, detection 8\nbecause nothing checks that the retrieved record matches the recipient. RPN around 216, and it is a\nseverity-9 row, so it leads regardless. \"CRM cache serves stale data\" lands in between. The right sprint\nis a cheap guard that asserts the recipient's account id equals the retrieved record's id, an eval that\nexercises near-duplicate names, and a freshness alert on the cache; failover can follow once the\ncatastrophic, undetected rows are contained. Show the table so the decision is about numbers, not\nopinions."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Detection score measure, and which direction is bad?","options":["How quickly the team responds after an incident is declared; low is bad","How likely current controls are to catch the failure before its effect reaches the user; high is bad","How many monitoring dashboards exist for the component; low is bad","How severe the failure is when it is finally noticed; high is bad"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Detection rates the controls that exist today, from 1 (certain to catch it) to 10 (no way to notice). The inversion trips people up: a high D multiplies the risk, which is why \"we have logs\" should not be scored as good detection unless someone is alerted by them."},{"q":"Why can sorting purely by RPN mislead, and what is the standard correction?","options":["RPN ignores occurrence, so frequent failures are under-ranked; weight occurrence double","RPN treats the factors as interchangeable, so a rare undetectable catastrophe can rank below a frequent cosmetic bug; gate on severity first, then sort by RPN","RPN only works for hardware, so software teams should use mean time between failures instead","RPN overweights detection, so detection should be dropped from the formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"S 10 × O 1 × D 1 is 10 while S 2 × O 5 × D 5 is 50, yet the first row is the one that ends the company. Severity-first ordering, as in the newer Action Priority approach, keeps catastrophic rows at the top."},{"q":"Which of these is correctly stated as a failure mode rather than an effect or a cause?","options":["The engineer forgot to add the tenant filter to the new retrieval client","A customer sees a competitor's pricing document in their answer","Retrieval returns documents belonging to another tenant","The vector database was upgraded last week"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A mode is the observable deviation in the function's output. The forgotten filter and the upgrade are causes; the customer seeing the document is the effect. Keeping the three apart is what makes occurrence, severity and detection scorable."}]},{"key":"reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","sources":["Basiri et al., 2016 — Chaos Engineering (IEEE Software)","Rosenthal & Jones, 2020 — Chaos Engineering: System Resiliency in Practice"],"why":"Fallback code is the least-tested code in any system. A team ships multi-provider failover and feels\nsafer. Six months later the primary provider degrades, the fallback fires for the first time in\nproduction, and fails: the secondary's API key expired, its model does not accept the same tool-call\nformat, or its rate limit is a tenth of the primary's. Every layer of resilience you add is another\nuntested path, and the testing you already do does not reach it. Unit tests mock the dependency. End-to-\nend tests run the happy path. Staging has neither production traffic patterns nor production data\nvolumes nor the real vendor quotas.\n\nThe naive alternative is to let real incidents validate the design. That works, in the sense that you\nlearn, but the lesson arrives at three in the morning, at full scale, uncontrolled, with customers\nwatching. Chaos engineering's founding observation is that the failure is going to happen anyway; the\nonly choice is whether it happens while you are watching, at one percent of traffic, with a kill switch,\nor later.\n\nTwo further problems push beyond \"test the fallback\". Distributed systems have emergent failure modes\nthat no component test predicts: retry storms, thundering herds on recovery, cache stampedes, cost\namplification when traffic falls to a pricier fallback model. Only exercising the whole system under\nfault reveals them. And systems drift: a fallback verified in March is unverified by June after three\nmodel upgrades and a vendor's quota change. Verification has to be repeated, which means it has to be\ncheap and automated.","how":"### Resilience testing: proving the mechanisms you built\nThis is the pre-production family, and it comes first because there is no point running experiments\nagainst mechanisms that do not exist yet.\n\n- **Fault injection** at the dependency boundary: make the provider client return 429s, 5xxs, 8-second\n  latencies or truncated completions for a configurable fraction of calls, and confirm that timeouts,\n  breakers, backoff and fallbacks behave as designed.\n- **Load, stress and soak tests**: load at expected peak to confirm SLOs hold; stress beyond peak to find\n  the knee where latency explodes and to see whether shedding engages; soak for hours at steady load to\n  catch leaks, including agent-specific ones such as conversation state that never expires or token\n  budgets that creep upward per session.\n- **Failover and recovery drills**: kill the primary database, region or model deployment on a schedule\n  and measure actual recovery time and data loss against the disaster-recovery targets.\n- **Continuous canaries for every fallback path**: a synthetic request through the secondary provider\n  every few minutes. This is the single cheapest control against fallback rot, because it catches\n  expired credentials, renamed models and quota changes before a real failover does.\n- **Game days**: scheduled, cross-team scenario exercises that include the humans: paging, runbooks,\n  communication, decision-making. A perfect failover with a confused on-call is still an outage.\n\n### Chaos engineering: discovering what you did not build for\nThe discipline is defined by its method, not by breaking things.\n\n1. **Define steady state as user-facing output.** Success rate, p95 latency, task completion rate, cost\n   per task: the SLIs. Not CPU or queue depth, because the hypothesis is about whether users notice.\n2. **State a falsifiable hypothesis.** \"If the primary provider returns 429 on 30 percent of calls,\n   success rate stays at or above 99 percent and p95 rises by less than 20 percent.\" A hypothesis makes\n   the result a finding either way; \"we killed a pod and nothing happened\" teaches nothing.\n3. **Inject realistic events.** Latency, errors, instance loss, network partition, dependency\n   unavailability, resource exhaustion, zone loss, clock skew. Prefer the events your FMEA ranked highest;\n   that table is the experiment backlog.\n4. **Minimize blast radius.** Start in staging, then one percent of production, an internal tenant, one\n   availability zone. Define an abort condition and a kill switch before starting. Run during business\n   hours with on-call informed, because an experiment that is indistinguishable from an incident is one.\n5. **Compare experiment and control.** Route a slice of traffic through the fault and an equal slice\n   without, and compare the SLIs, as in an A/B test. This separates the fault's effect from background\n   noise.\n6. **Fix, then widen, then automate.** Each refuted hypothesis becomes a fix and a regression test. Verified\n   hypotheses graduate to larger blast radius and eventually to continuous automated runs, because the\n   system will drift away from the verified state.\n\n### Injection mechanisms\nApplication-level hooks in your own provider and tool clients are the most useful for AI systems: you\ncannot make a vendor's API go down, but you can make your client behave as if it had. Network-level\ntools add latency, bandwidth caps and connection resets through a proxy (Toxiproxy is a common choice)\nor a service mesh's fault-injection rules. Infrastructure-level tools kill instances and pods, fill disks\nand hog CPU; this is the Chaos Monkey lineage, continued by Kubernetes-native projects such as\nLitmusChaos and Chaos Mesh, commercial platforms, and the cloud providers' own fault-injection services.\nData-level injection is specific to AI and usually hand-built: serve a stale index snapshot, drop a\nfraction of retrieved chunks, revoke a tool's credentials, or return refusals, truncated text and\nmalformed JSON from a model stub.\n\n### What is different about LLM and agent systems\nFaults are semantic as well as operational. A model returns plausible nonsense; retrieval returns the\nwrong tenant's documents; a tool reports success with wrong data. Infrastructure chaos never produces\nthese, so the experiment must inject them and the hypothesis must be about whether guards (schema\nvalidation, groundedness checks, confirmation steps) catch them. Amplification is the second difference:\ninjecting 429s should reveal whether backoff, jitter and step budgets hold, or whether an agent loops\nfifty times and a retry storm forms. The third is cost: a fault that pushes traffic to a fallback model\nhas a dollar blast radius as well as a latency one, so experiments need a spending cap and the cost SLI\nbelongs in the hypothesis.","example":"A fault-injecting wrapper around the real model client, controlled by environment variables so it is\ninert by default, plus a minimal experiment runner with an abort condition. The experiment is run once\nas a control and once with a fault, and the two results are compared against the hypothesis.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os, random, time\n\nclass RateLimitError(Exception): pass\n\nclass ChaosLLM:\n    \"\"\"Wraps the real client. Injects faults on a fraction of calls; inert unless CHAOS_RATE is set.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, client):\n        self.client = client\n        self.rate = float(os.getenv(\"CHAOS_RATE\", \"0\"))\n        self.mode = os.getenv(\"CHAOS_MODE\", \"latency\")\n\n    async def complete(self, **kw):\n        if random.random() < self.rate:\n            if self.mode == \"latency\":\n                await asyncio.sleep(8.0)                 # provider at 8 s: do timeouts and breakers fire?\n            elif self.mode == \"rate_limit\":\n                raise RateLimitError(\"429\")              # do backoff, jitter and step budgets hold?\n            elif self.mode == \"garbage\":\n                return {\"content\": \"{not json\", \"usage\": {\"total_tokens\": 5}}   # does validation catch it?\n        return await self.client.complete(**kw)\n\nasync def experiment(handler, requests, min_success=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Runs traffic through `handler`, aborts if steady state is violated, reports the SLIs.\"\"\"\n    latencies, ok = [], 0\n    for req in requests:\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            await handler(req); ok += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        latencies.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n        if len(latencies) >= 20 and ok / len(latencies) < min_success:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"steady state violated; experiment aborted\")   # the kill switch\n    latencies.sort()\n    return {\"success_rate\": ok / len(latencies),\n            \"p95_s\": latencies[int(0.95 * (len(latencies) - 1))]}\n\n# Control run: CHAOS_RATE=0. Experiment: CHAOS_MODE=rate_limit CHAOS_RATE=0.3.\n# Hypothesis: success_rate >= 0.99 and p95_s < 1.2 x the control's. Anything else is a finding.\n```\n\nIn practice the same wrapper sits in the deployed service behind a per-tenant or per-request flag, so\nthe fault can be applied to one percent of real traffic; the runner's abort check becomes an alert on\nthe experiment slice's SLIs that flips the flag off.","tradeoffs":["Production experiments give true signal, with real traffic, data and vendor limits, at real risk; staging is safe but misses emergent behavior. Start in staging and graduate to production only once abort conditions, observability per slice and a kill switch exist.","Every experiment costs engineering time and sometimes vendor dollars, because faults push traffic to fallbacks and retries; prioritize by FMEA risk rather than by what is easy to inject.","Continuous automated chaos catches drift but demands a mature on-call and can be mistaken for a real incident; scheduled game days are cheaper and clearer but only test what someone remembered to schedule.","Semantic fault injection tests the guards that matter for AI but needs trustworthy evals to judge success; infrastructure fault injection is easy to measure but blind to wrong-answer failures. Do both, in that order of importance for an LLM product."],"pitfalls":["Running without a steady-state hypothesis. The symptom: a log of experiments none of which produced a fix, and nobody able to say what was verified.","No abort condition or kill switch. The symptom: a postmortem whose root cause is the chaos experiment itself.","Injecting only infrastructure faults into an AI system. The symptom: pods survive every drill, then the first real 429 storm triggers agent loops and a cost spike nobody tested for.","Never canarying the fallback. The symptom: the first real failover fails for a configuration reason, an expired key, a renamed model, a lower quota, that a five-minute synthetic check would have caught.","Experimenting without per-slice observability. The symptom: the hypothesis cannot be evaluated because the experiment's traffic is indistinguishable from everything else in the dashboards.","Applying faults to shared customer traffic without regard to contracts. The symptom: a customer's SLA breached by your own experiment, and a difficult conversation about who caused the outage.","Ignoring the cost blast radius. The symptom: a full-traffic primary-failure drill that routes an hour of traffic to the expensive fallback model, visible on the next invoice."],"when":"Begin once the basic mechanisms exist and observability can show SLIs for an experiment slice; before\nthat, build the mechanisms. A sensible progression: dependency fault injection in staging for the top\nthree FMEA rows, synthetic canaries through every fallback path, one game day per quarter for failover\nand disaster recovery, then production chaos at one percent for the most critical mechanisms, growing\ntoward automation. Do not bother for a prototype or a service with no SLO, because there is no steady\nstate to defend. Measure hypotheses verified and refuted, weaknesses found per experiment, time to\ndetect and time to abort. In interviews, describe one experiment end to end: steady state, hypothesis,\nfault, blast radius, abort condition, result, and the fix it produced.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the fault-injection hooks into provider and tool clients, writes the experiment and its abort check, and turns every refuted hypothesis into a regression test.","architect":"Decides which failure modes must be proven rather than assumed before launch, sets the blast-radius and approval policy, and ties experiments to SLOs and the FMEA backlog.","fde":"Runs a game day with the customer before go-live, cutting their SSO or ERP connector to show the agent degrading gracefully, so their operations team has seen the behavior and the runbook works in their environment."},"check":{"q":"You have shipped multi-provider fallback for an agent. How would you prove it works without\nwaiting for the primary provider to fail, and what would you watch for?","a":"Three layers. First, a synthetic canary every few minutes through the secondary path, which\ncatches expired keys, renamed models and quota changes continuously. Second, staging fault injection:\nforce 100 percent 503s from the primary through the client hook with the hypothesis that success rate\nstays at or above 99 percent, p95 stays within budget and cost per request stays under twice the\ncontrol; watch specifically for tool-call format incompatibilities, differing context limits and\nschema differences between the two models. Third, production chaos on one percent of an internal\ntenant's traffic with 30 percent injected 429s, aborting if success drops below 98 percent. Throughout,\nwatch retry amplification (provider calls per user request), fallback rate, breaker state transitions\nand spend, and sample the fallback outputs through your quality evals because the secondary model may be\nmeasurably worse. Finally test recovery: when the primary returns, traffic should move back through the\nbreaker's half-open probes without a thundering herd."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes chaos engineering from ordinary fault-injection testing?","options":["Chaos engineering only runs in production, while fault injection only runs in staging","A falsifiable steady-state hypothesis measured on user-facing SLIs, a bounded blast radius, and comparison against a control","Chaos engineering uses random faults, while fault injection uses chosen ones","Chaos engineering requires a dedicated tool such as Chaos Monkey"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fault injection is a mechanism; chaos engineering is an experimental method built on it. The hypothesis is what turns \"we broke it and watched\" into a finding, and the blast radius and control group are what make running it on real systems defensible."},{"q":"Which class of fault is most specific to AI systems and most often skipped in drills?","options":["Killing a pod in the inference deployment","Losing an availability zone","Semantic faults such as plausible-but-wrong output, malformed JSON and stale or cross-tenant retrieval","Filling the disk on a worker node"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Infrastructure faults are easy to inject and measure, but an LLM product's worst failures are silent wrong answers. Only injecting those, via model and retrieval stubs, tests whether validation, grounding checks and confirmation steps actually catch them."},{"q":"Why run a synthetic request through the fallback provider continuously rather than only during drills?","options":["To keep the fallback provider's cache warm for lower latency","Because vendors require minimum usage to keep an account active","Fallback paths rot through credential expiry, model renames and quota changes, and a canary detects that before a real failover does","To accumulate usage data for negotiating a better contract"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fallback carries no traffic in normal operation, so nothing exercises it between drills. A cheap periodic request turns months of silent decay into an alert within minutes of the configuration breaking."}]},{"key":"reliability/slas-slos","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer et al., 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook","Hidalgo, 2020 — Implementing Service Level Objectives"],"why":"Without explicit targets, reliability is argued by anecdote. Product wants features, operations wants\nstability, every incident is a crisis and no incident is ever acceptable. \"100 percent reliable\" is not a\ntarget: it is impossible, each additional nine costs roughly an order of magnitude more, and past a point\nusers cannot perceive the difference because their own network and devices fail more often than you do.\nAn SLO makes the trade explicit. Pick the number users actually need, measure it from their side, and\ntreat the remainder as a budget to be spent on velocity: deploys, model upgrades, experiments. When the\nbudget is gone, reliability work takes priority. That policy, not the number, is what changes behavior.\n\nSLAs exist for a different reason. Enterprise customers need a promise with teeth before procurement\nwill sign, so the SLA states a target and the credit paid if it is missed. The internal SLO must be\nstricter than the SLA, because the gap is the margin in which you detect and fix problems before they\nbecome contract events; a team whose SLO equals its SLA has legal in every incident review.\n\nAI systems add a problem the classic framework did not have to solve. The traditional availability SLI,\nthe share of non-5xx responses, is nearly meaningless for a model endpoint: a confident wrong answer in\n800 milliseconds counts as a success. An SLO built only on that will stay green while customers churn.\nUseful SLIs for an LLM product must cover latency in the shape users feel it, correctness or quality as\nmeasured by evals, and often cost, and each of those can be broken by a vendor changing a model without\na single line of your code changing.","how":"### The three layers, precisely\nAn **SLI** is a ratio: good events divided by valid events. \"Good\" must be defined in one sentence that a\nquery can implement, for example status 2xx *and* total latency under 3 seconds *and* output passed\nschema validation. \"Valid\" excludes what should not count, such as malformed client requests, but be\ncareful: excluding too much hides real failures. Measure as close to the user as you can, at the load\nbalancer or in the client, because the server cannot count requests it never received.\n\nAn **SLO** is a target plus a window: 99.9 percent of valid requests good over a rolling 30 days. Rolling\nwindows beat calendar months because a calendar window resets everyone's attention on the first and\nexhausts by the twentieth. A service usually carries several SLOs: availability, latency, and for AI\nproducts a quality SLO.\n\nAn **SLA** is a subset of the SLOs, looser, legally reviewed, with defined remedies and exclusions\n(scheduled maintenance, customer-caused load). A common shape is an internal SLO of 99.9 percent behind\nan external SLA of 99.5 percent.\n\n### Error budget arithmetic\nBudget = (1 − SLO) × window. For 99.9 percent over 30 days: 30 × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes, times 0.001,\ngives 43.2 minutes of full outage, or 1,000 bad requests per million. The whole table is just this\nmultiplication: 99 percent allows 7.2 hours per 30 days, 99.5 percent 3.6 hours, 99.9 percent 43.2\nminutes, 99.95 percent 21.6 minutes, 99.99 percent 4.32 minutes. The last row is why 99.99 percent\nrequires automated failover: a human cannot be paged, understand the problem and act in four minutes.\n\nDependencies cap what you can promise. If your request path serially needs a provider with a 99.9\npercent SLA and a vector store with 99.95 percent, the product of the two is about 99.85 percent before\nyour own code fails once. Your SLO cannot honestly exceed that ceiling without redundancy; an\nindependent fallback provider raises the provider term toward 1 − (0.001 × 0.001), though true\nindependence is rare and the figure is illustrative.\n\n### Burn rate and alerting\nBurn rate is how fast you are consuming the budget relative to the pace that would exhaust it exactly\nat the end of the window. A burn rate of 1 spends the whole budget in 30 days; a burn rate of 10 spends\nit in 3 days. Alerting on burn rate, rather than on a raw error-rate threshold, is the point: a\ntwo-minute blip at 5 percent errors consumes a sliver of budget and should not page anyone, while a\npersistent 0.3 percent error rate on a 99.9 percent SLO is a 3× burn that will breach the SLO in ten\ndays and should. The widely used multi-window scheme from the SRE Workbook pages when the burn rate over\nthe last hour exceeds 14.4 (that consumes 2 percent of a 30-day budget in an hour) or over six hours\nexceeds 6 (5 percent of budget), and opens a ticket when it exceeds 1 over three days (10 percent). Each\nlong window is paired with a short one so the alert clears once the problem stops.\n\n### The error budget policy\nThe numbers do nothing by themselves. The policy says what happens when the budget is spent: freeze\nrisky launches, require review for prompt and model changes, prioritize reliability work until the\nbudget recovers; and what happens when it is plentiful: ship faster, run chaos experiments, which spend\nbudget on purpose. Agreeing this in advance with product leadership is most of the work.\n\n### SLIs for AI systems\n- **Latency**, in the shape users feel: time to first token for streaming responses; total time per\n  response; for agents, time to task completion, which can be minutes and needs its own target.\n- **Availability** after resilience: the fraction of requests that complete without a provider or tool\n  error once retries and fallbacks have had their chance.\n- **Quality**: the fraction of a sampled slice of responses that pass automated evals (groundedness,\n  schema, policy), with a smaller human-labeled sample as calibration. Sampling is used because\n  evaluating every response costs as much as serving it. A quality SLO is the one most likely to move\n  without a deploy, which is why model version pinning and evaluation gates exist.\n- **Cost** per successful task, paired with quality so that neither can be gamed by sacrificing the other.\n- **Side-effect correctness** for agents: actions reconciled against intent, such as tickets created\n  equals tickets requested.\n\nNote what vendor SLAs cover: usually availability only, with service credits. They do not promise\nlatency or quality, so your latency and quality SLOs must assume you absorb those risks yourself.","example":"A worked month for an assistant with an SLO of 99.9 percent \"good\" responses over a rolling 30 days,\nwhere good means 2xx, total latency under 4 seconds, and schema-valid output. Traffic is 20 million\nrequests per month, so the budget is 20,000 bad responses, equivalent to 43.2 minutes of total outage.\nAverage traffic is 20,000,000 ÷ 43,200 minutes, about 463 requests per minute.\n\nOn day 6 the provider degrades for 25 minutes and 60 percent of requests in that window fail or time\nout. Bad responses: 25 × 463 × 0.6, about 6,945, which is 35 percent of the month's budget gone in 25\nminutes. The burn rate during the incident is 0.6 ÷ 0.001 = 600, so the one-hour 14.4× page fires within\nthe first few minutes, which is exactly right. Afterwards 13,055 bad responses remain for 24 days.\n\nOn day 9 a prompt edit raises schema-validation failures from 0.02 percent to 0.15 percent, persistently.\nThat is a bad fraction of 0.0015 against an allowed 0.001: a burn rate of 1.5. Nothing is on fire, no\npage fires, but at 463 × 0.0015 ≈ 0.69 bad responses per minute the remaining budget lasts 13,055 ÷ 0.69\n≈ 18,800 minutes, about 13 days, so the SLO will be breached before the window closes. The three-day 1×\nticket alert catches it on day 12. The error budget policy now applies: the prompt change is rolled back\nor guarded with a repair step, and model upgrades are frozen until the rolling budget recovers. The\nexternal SLA of 99.5 percent, allowing 100,000 bad responses, was never close to breach; the internal SLO\ndid its job of forcing action two weeks before a customer could have noticed.","tradeoffs":["A tighter SLO pleases users but costs roughly an order of magnitude more per extra nine, in redundancy and in lost velocity from a smaller budget; it stops paying off once users' own client reliability is worse than yours.","Measuring SLIs at the edge or client is truthful but includes noise you do not control; server-side measurement is clean but blind to the failures users feel. Use the edge for availability and the server for latency breakdowns.","Request-based SLOs weight by traffic, so a ten-minute outage at peak costs more than one at night; time-based SLOs (\"minutes with error rate under x\") treat them equally. Request-based reflects user impact better; time-based is simpler to put in a contract.","Quality SLIs via sampled automated evals are affordable but noisy and judge-dependent; full human review is accurate but too slow and expensive for a 30-day window. The sample size sets the confidence, so size it to the decision you need to make.","Signing an SLA unlocks enterprise deals but makes every dependency's SLA your ceiling and converts internal misses into contract events. Worth it when revenue justifies buying the redundancy."],"pitfalls":["Defining the SLI as \"not a 5xx\". The symptom: a green SLO dashboard during a quality incident while support tickets climb.","Measuring inside the service. The symptom: the service reports 99.99 percent while the load balancer shows 2 percent 502s during every deploy.","Using mean latency as the SLI. The symptom: p50 is fine while a tenth of users wait 20 seconds; use the fraction of requests under a threshold instead.","Calendar-month windows. The symptom: relaxed attention on the first, a burned budget by the twentieth, every month.","Setting the SLO equal to the SLA. The symptom: legal joins engineering incident reviews and every internal miss becomes a credit negotiation.","Having no error budget policy. The symptom: the budget is exhausted three months running and nothing about how the team ships has changed.","Alerting on raw error-rate thresholds. The symptom: pages at three in the morning for two-minute blips that consumed a fraction of a percent of budget, while a slow burn that will breach the SLO goes unnoticed for a week.","Assuming a vendor's SLA covers what your SLO needs. The symptom: the provider \"met its SLA\" during a ten-fold latency degradation that blew your latency SLO, because their SLA only promises availability.","Setting a quality SLO without pinning model versions. The symptom: the eval pass rate drops with no deploy in the changelog, and the cause is a silent vendor model update."],"when":"Every user-facing service needs at least one availability SLO and one latency SLO; an AI service adds a\nquality SLO as soon as it has an eval it trusts, and a cost-per-task target soon after. Set the first\nSLO from measured history, slightly below current performance, rather than from aspiration, then tighten\nwith evidence. Sign SLAs only for SLIs you can measure from the customer's vantage point and that your\ndependencies' SLAs can support. Day to day this looks like checking budget remaining in sprint planning,\nburn-rate alerts on the pager, and a monthly SLO review. In interviews, define the three terms crisply,\ncompute an error budget from a target and a window without hesitation, explain why burn-rate alerting\nbeats threshold alerting, and propose SLIs for an LLM product that go beyond uptime.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments good and valid counters at the edge, builds the eval sampling pipeline for the quality SLI, implements multi-window burn-rate alerts, and checks the budget before risky changes.","architect":"Chooses SLOs per service tier, derives them from dependency SLAs, decides where redundancy is required to meet them, and owns the error budget policy agreed with product.","fde":"Translates a customer's SLA request into SLIs measurable inside their environment, explains why 99.99 percent needs redundancy they have not bought, and builds the shared dashboard that governs hypercare."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for a 99.95 percent availability SLA on your agent product. Your LLM provider\noffers a 99.9 percent SLA, your vector database 99.95 percent, and you have no fallback provider. How\ndo you respond, and what would have to change?","a":"Start with the ceiling. The request path needs both dependencies, so availability is bounded by\nroughly 0.999 × 0.9995 ≈ 0.9985, about 99.85 percent, before any failure of your own code. Signing\n99.95 percent, whose budget is 21.6 minutes per 30 days, would be a promise one provider incident breaks.\nThe honest response offers 99.5 percent contractually with an internal SLO of 99.9 percent now, and a\npath to 99.95 percent priced to include what it costs: an independent fallback provider, which lifts the\nprovider term toward 1 − 0.001² if the two are truly independent, a vector-store replica, and a\ndefinition of \"available\" that counts degraded-mode answers from cache or fallback. Add exclusions for\nscheduled maintenance and customer-caused overload, and measure from their side of the network. Finally\nclarify scope: an availability SLA says nothing about latency or answer quality, so propose separate\nlatency and quality SLOs, visible on a shared dashboard but not under penalty until both sides trust\nthe measurement."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the error budget for a 99.9 percent SLO over a 30-day window, expressed as full-outage time?","options":["4.32 minutes","43.2 minutes","7.2 hours","432 minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"30 days is 43,200 minutes; 0.1 percent of that is 43.2 minutes. The 4.32-minute figure belongs to 99.99 percent and 7.2 hours to 99 percent; the arithmetic is the same multiplication each time."},{"q":"Why should the internal SLO be stricter than the external SLA?","options":["Because SLAs are measured over longer windows than SLOs","Because customers expect the published number to be rounded up","The gap is the margin in which problems are detected and fixed before they become contract events with penalties","Because vendors require it in their own terms of service"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the SLO equals the SLA, every internal miss is already a breach and legal enters every incident review. A stricter SLO forces action while there is still budget left before the contractual line."},{"q":"Why alert on error-budget burn rate rather than on a raw error-rate threshold?","options":["Because error rates cannot be computed in real time","Burn-rate alerts page only when the budget is genuinely threatened, catching both fast outages and slow persistent degradation","Because burn rate is required for SLA reporting","Because raw error rates are always noisier than burn rates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A two-minute blip consumes a sliver of budget and should not wake anyone; a persistent 0.3 percent error rate on a 99.9 percent SLO burns at 3× and will breach within the window. Multi-window burn-rate alerts distinguish the two; a single threshold cannot."}]}]});
